WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.208
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4620.

2
00:00:01.208 --> 00:00:02.416
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.416 --> 00:00:02.818
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright.

4
00:00:02.818 --> 00:00:05.234
Today is May twelfth, Thursday, 2016.

5
00:00:05.234 --> 00:00:19.325
I'm here with Mr. Delma Bennett and his wife Ms. Christine Bennett and we're here today to talk about the history of Mossville in a project we're conducting in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum.

6
00:00:19.325 --> 00:00:25.364
Alright and the first question: Could you both state your full name for the recording?

7
00:00:25.364 --> 00:00:27.779
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>My name is Christine Delafosse Bennett.

8
00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:31.000
<v DELMA BENNETT>And my name is Delma Leroy Bennett.

9
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.763
<v ARSENEAULT>And when and where were you born?

10
00:00:35.763 --> 00:00:50.052
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>When you ask where I was born I would tell them in Mossville, but I was born in a hospital [laughs].

11
00:00:50.052 --> 00:00:58.216
I was born in Lake Charles, Louisiana at Memorial Hospital in 1953.

12
00:00:58.216 --> 00:01:04.340
<v DELMA BENNETT>I was born January seventh, 1944 San Francisco, California.

13
00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.423
<v ARSENEAULT>And what are your parents' names?

14
00:01:08.423 --> 00:01:13.186
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>My father name is [Harden?] Louis Delafosse.

15
00:01:13.186 --> 00:01:17.948
My mother name is Mabel Paul Delafosse.

16
00:01:17.948 --> 00:01:27.474
<v DELMA BENNETT>My mother name is Louise Braud Bennett and my father name is Leroy Bennett.

17
00:01:27.474 --> 00:01:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And what brought them to . . . Well yours are probably still in San Francisco?

18
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:37.494
<v DELMA BENNETT>Ma'am?

19
00:01:37.494 --> 00:01:43.917
<v ARSENEAULT>I was wondering what brought them to . . . I was going to say Mossville.

20
00:01:43.917 --> 00:01:44.411
Mossville.

21
00:01:44.411 --> 00:01:46.881
What brought them to Mossville?

22
00:01:46.881 --> 00:01:54.292
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Well my parents lived in Lake Charles for the first three kids of the twelve.

23
00:01:54.292 --> 00:01:57.256
My father had a child before.

24
00:01:57.256 --> 00:02:14.548
But my mother and father lived in Lake Charles and then they decided that they . . . well his . . . My dad's father was there and they had land and they offered my dad to move to Mossville.

25
00:02:14.548 --> 00:02:19.982
That was like sixty-some years ago that they moved to Mossville.

26
00:02:19.982 --> 00:02:22.946
I don't know exactly the year.

27
00:02:22.946 --> 00:02:24.923
I had it down.

28
00:02:24.923 --> 00:02:29.863
But they only moved there because his father was there.

29
00:02:29.863 --> 00:02:40.238
My father's father had gotten land and was making his stop right there in Mossville because they're originally was from Kinder.

30
00:02:40.238 --> 00:02:48.143
They moved from Kinder . . . The dad went to Mossville, and my father went to Lake Charles.

31
00:02:48.143 --> 00:02:52.095
Then my father winded up moving to Mossville.

32
00:02:52.095 --> 00:03:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he ever talk about first impressions of Mossville, or what it was like moving?

33
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.120
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Well my dad said that it was very, very quiet and it was country living.

34
00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:06.936
That's all.

35
00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:08.976
Just a little country town.

36
00:03:08.976 --> 00:03:11.017
It wasn't large at all.

37
00:03:11.017 --> 00:03:14.689
And he said that everybody lived off the land.

38
00:03:14.689 --> 00:03:19.177
They either had gardens and they dug wells for their water,

39
00:03:19.177 --> 00:03:26.113
women washed their clothes and hung them on the line, and everything was just not modern living.

40
00:03:26.113 --> 00:03:29.785
It was . . . They didn't call it hard back then.

41
00:03:29.785 --> 00:03:32.233
They knew how to make it.

42
00:03:32.233 --> 00:03:39.986
So they lived off the land and most of their living was through helping one another in the community.

43
00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:44.066
So he always felt that they would never leave Mossville.

44
00:03:44.066 --> 00:03:48.146
So he winded up with . . . Let' see, he had ten.

45
00:03:48.146 --> 00:03:48.962
Ten more.

46
00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:51.002
They came with my sister.

47
00:03:51.002 --> 00:03:58.347
My oldest sister was a baby when they moved to Mossville, and they had ten more after that.

48
00:03:58.347 --> 00:04:06.099
We all were born and raised right there in Mossville and my father did a lot of the wells.

49
00:04:06.099 --> 00:04:10.587
Helped with a lot of digging of the wells in Mossville.

50
00:04:10.587 --> 00:04:18.340
We had well water and everybody was like . . . People make it seem like we were one big happy family,

51
00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.828
but people that lived in Mossville . . . We all knew each other.

52
00:04:22.828 --> 00:04:33.436
We were familiar with one another, but we weren't the type of people that lived up at each other house and, "Oh we got along great."

53
00:04:33.436 --> 00:04:40.373
No, we lived in a community that everything that we did we looked out for each other.

54
00:04:40.373 --> 00:04:48.533
We had the little school there, Mossville School, and it was a school before we because we were younger ones.

55
00:04:48.533 --> 00:04:50.981
So that was another school there.

56
00:04:50.981 --> 00:04:55.877
They did a lot of schools that I didn't know anything about.

57
00:04:55.877 --> 00:05:03.630
The only time I got to . . . When I was born in 1970 . . . 1953 the school hadn't been built then.

58
00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:08.934
So I would say that my sister who was there as a baby,

59
00:05:08.934 --> 00:05:19.950
she was one of the first ones in our family . . . Well she was one of the ones that finished high school there before they integrated the school.

60
00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:20.767
By time

61
00:05:20.767 --> 00:05:30.559
<c.p>I got to the . . . I went to the elementary school there and we wind up being integrated when I was in the ninth grade.

62
00:05:30.559 --> 00:05:33.823
They wound up moving us to Westlake High.

63
00:05:33.823 --> 00:05:37.087
<v ARSENEAULT>I definitely want to talk more about that.

64
00:05:37.087 --> 00:05:38.719
That's a really good . . .

65
00:05:38.719 --> 00:05:39.943
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>That was horrible.

66
00:05:39.943 --> 00:05:40.351
<v ARSENEAULT>Integration . . .

67
00:05:40.351 --> 00:05:41.575
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Oh my God.

68
00:05:41.575 --> 00:05:42.800
That was horrible.

69
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.512
And I laugh and I tell my kids I missed out on my education.

70
00:05:48.512 --> 00:05:53.000
My four high school years from ninth grade to twelfth.

71
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.043
<v ARSENEAULT>What school did you have to . . .?

72
00:05:55.043 --> 00:05:58.106
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>I left Mossville to go to Westlake High School.

73
00:05:58.106 --> 00:05:59.128
<v ARSENEAULT>Westlake High School.

74
00:05:59.128 --> 00:06:02.191
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>I'll never forget the day why . . . We begged them.

75
00:06:02.191 --> 00:06:04.574
Why couldn't Westlake come to our school?

76
00:06:04.574 --> 00:06:09.000
Why were they taking us from our school and bringing us to Westlake?

77
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:10.702
We didn't have no argument.

78
00:06:10.702 --> 00:06:12.064
It was just out.

79
00:06:12.064 --> 00:06:17.170
Then after we got to Westlake High and . . . Do I say just really what happened?

80
00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:19.213
You want me to say that?

81
00:06:19.213 --> 00:06:27.043
When we pulled up on the bus at that school some of the white kids say, "Here come that bus load of niggers."

82
00:06:27.043 --> 00:06:27.723
That destroyed

83
00:06:27.723 --> 00:06:28.064
<c.p>us.

84
00:06:28.064 --> 00:06:35.213
Because we didn't want to go at first so then we . . . I had to wind up fighting the whole four years.

85
00:06:35.213 --> 00:06:39.979
Going to jail, being kicked out of school, fighting every day because they say,

86
00:06:39.979 --> 00:06:45.766
"Y'all not going to come here thinking this is y'alls school because this is not y'alls school.

87
00:06:45.766 --> 00:06:48.830
Y'all need to go back where y'all came from."

88
00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:54.277
Well that's children fighting against each other, but that's not older people letting us know that,

89
00:06:54.277 --> 00:06:58.021
or letting those kids know that we didn't have a choice.

90
00:06:58.021 --> 00:07:01.085
We either go, because they're shutting our school down.

91
00:07:01.085 --> 00:07:04.489
So when we got there we couldn't be on nothing.

92
00:07:04.489 --> 00:07:10.617
We had our school where we were . . . Had our football team, cheerleaders, everything we had at our school.

93
00:07:10.617 --> 00:07:16.745
But when we went to Westlake High they said we had to work our way up to that.

94
00:07:16.745 --> 00:07:25.255
Well we began to march and let them know that, "We want just what we had at our school because this is our school now."

95
00:07:25.255 --> 00:07:28.660
This ain't just their school anymore it's our school, too.

96
00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.362
We're going to be one.

97
00:07:30.362 --> 00:07:32.745
We're going to act like we’re one.

98
00:07:32.745 --> 00:07:40.915
So we ain't got to work up to nothing because we had already worked up to being football players, cheerleaders, and student council members.

99
00:07:40.915 --> 00:07:41.936
So they wouldn't.

100
00:07:41.936 --> 00:07:46.702
So every day we had a fight for somebody calling us out our name,

101
00:07:46.702 --> 00:07:50.447
or picking at us, or . . . We had had a rough time.

102
00:07:50.447 --> 00:08:01.000
So by having to fight your way for everything the only way they were going to stop the fighting at the school was to allow us . . . [phone ringing] That's bad?

103
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:02.032
<v ARSENEAULT>No, it's okay.

104
00:08:02.032 --> 00:08:06.851
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Was to allow us to immediately start letting us be on the student council.

105
00:08:06.851 --> 00:08:09.260
Letting us make decisions for the school.

106
00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:13.045
The colors . . . and we didn't get a chance to do that.

107
00:08:13.045 --> 00:08:16.831
Their colors was orange and black; ours was maroon and gold.

108
00:08:16.831 --> 00:08:21.994
So we didn't get a chance to have a chance to pick our school color.

109
00:08:21.994 --> 00:08:25.779
Take our school color with us and say, "Let’s not have

110
00:08:25.779 --> 00:08:26.812
<c.p>orange and black.

111
00:08:26.812 --> 00:08:28.532
Let’s have black and maroon."

112
00:08:28.532 --> 00:08:29.909
No, that was out.

113
00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:34.727
So it's a lot of things they compromised with us with that we accepted,

114
00:08:34.727 --> 00:08:41.266
but eventually as it took four years for us to be able to get a part of that school.

115
00:08:41.266 --> 00:08:47.805
That was my four years of missing out on my education because if I heard it was a fight,

116
00:08:47.805 --> 00:08:51.591
or they were beating up on somebody I left class because . . .

117
00:08:51.591 --> 00:08:54.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You said you went to jail?

118
00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.990
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Well they would pick us . . . Well what they happen,

119
00:08:56.990 --> 00:09:05.628
they would call the police on us and they would call our parents and tell them they were fixing to take us to jail for fighting.

120
00:09:05.628 --> 00:09:09.947
Well by time our parents got there the police would let us go.

121
00:09:09.947 --> 00:09:11.940
So a lot of things happened.

122
00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:16.259
During the racial time there was a little store in Mossville called Rougeau's.

123
00:09:16.259 --> 00:09:17.920
<v ARSENEAULT>How do you spell that?

124
00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:19.249
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>That's a good one.

125
00:09:19.249 --> 00:09:20.578
How you spell Rougeau's?

126
00:09:20.578 --> 00:09:21.575
I don’t know.

127
00:09:21.575 --> 00:09:23.236
I know it's an R.

128
00:09:23.236 --> 00:09:23.568
<v DELMA BENNETT>R-O-U-G . . .

129
00:09:23.568 --> 00:09:23.900
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Yeah.

130
00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.558
Rougeau's was a little white store in Mossville.

131
00:09:26.558 --> 00:09:27.887
Very beautiful white people.

132
00:09:27.887 --> 00:09:28.551
Very beautiful.

133
00:09:28.551 --> 00:09:38.518
Well one day I went in to Rougeau's and I had to get some of the thread for my outfit I was making in home ec [home economics] in school.

134
00:09:38.518 --> 00:09:42.505
Well we still at Mossville in the home ec class for sewing.

135
00:09:42.505 --> 00:09:45.827
I went to match my thread with my dress color;

136
00:09:45.827 --> 00:09:48.485
<c.p>the material that I had in the bag.

137
00:09:48.485 --> 00:09:56.126
This black guy who was working in the store with them . . . The lady said . . . the white lady asked him did he see anything.

138
00:09:56.126 --> 00:09:59.116
He said, "Yeah, I think she stole a thread."

139
00:09:59.116 --> 00:10:03.103
I said, "I didn't steal no thread I don't have to steal.

140
00:10:03.103 --> 00:10:04.100
I have money."

141
00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:07.090
The white lady said, "Well give me your bag."

142
00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:09.748
I said, "I'm not giving you my bag."

143
00:10:09.748 --> 00:10:14.731
Well they jerked the bag from me and threw my stuff out on the counter.

144
00:10:14.731 --> 00:10:18.053
And he said, "She didn't have no thread in there."

145
00:10:18.053 --> 00:10:21.375
I said, "And you won't have this store anymore either.

146
00:10:21.375 --> 00:10:27.355
We done work with you all these years and you have the audacity to accuse me for stealing?

147
00:10:27.355 --> 00:10:34.000
As good as we've been here in this community with you as a white person in a black neighborhood?"

148
00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:41.015
So she . . . we went . . . they called the paddy wagon on us because we marched for one week to get them out of there.

149
00:10:41.015 --> 00:10:47.725
My brother went in . . . my brother and some more of his friends went in the store and they asked for a popsicle.

150
00:10:47.725 --> 00:10:51.690
And they said, "Well what color you want?' He said, "I don't know.

151
00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:52.910
Where the popsicles at?"

152
00:10:52.910 --> 00:11:01.145
Well the lady knew . . . Didn't know that that was my brothers just coming back later on to find out was that black guy going to test him.

153
00:11:01.145 --> 00:11:06.025
Well of course we always said he was . . . not prejudice but we call him Uncle Tom.

154
00:11:06.025 --> 00:11:10.600
He went along with whatever the white people did just because he wanted a job.

155
00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:16.395
So my brother asked for a popsicle and then after that they asked him for what color he wanted.

156
00:11:16.395 --> 00:11:21.885
He said, "I don't know let me see where the popsicle . . . “ He said, “I think I want red."

157
00:11:21.885 --> 00:11:24.325
He said, "No, I think I want green."

158
00:11:24.325 --> 00:11:29.815
He kept changing his mind and the man said, "No, you need to get up out of here."

159
00:11:29.815 --> 00:11:35.000
And he . . . They had a little passing licks and that's really what made us start picketing.

160
00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:39.879
We start picketing the store and we marched out there more than a week.

161
00:11:39.879 --> 00:11:48.591
I think it was almost three weeks we marched and wouldn't allow people to come and shop with her until they closed the store down.

162
00:11:48.591 --> 00:11:52.076
We wound up going to jail in the paddy wagon.

163
00:11:52.076 --> 00:11:54.167
They took us all to jail.

164
00:11:54.167 --> 00:12:00.439
There was about ten of us that went to jail fighting for our rights of being respected as

165
00:12:00.439 --> 00:12:00.788
<c.p>people.

166
00:12:00.788 --> 00:12:03.227
Not black or white, but as people.

167
00:12:03.227 --> 00:12:08.455
So the same thing we had to go through in our school in Westlake High,

168
00:12:08.455 --> 00:12:12.636
but when they closed our school they didn't give us a choice.

169
00:12:12.636 --> 00:12:17.864
They didn’t . . . At that time whatever the whites say that's what had to be done.

170
00:12:17.864 --> 00:12:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any teachers at Westlake High?

171
00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.431
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>The black and the white teachers?

172
00:12:23.431 --> 00:12:26.267
<v ARSENEAULT>Any teacher that stands out in your . . .?

173
00:12:26.267 --> 00:12:34.369
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Well Ms. Rogers who's still . . . We call her our teacher today because we still go to church where she goes.

174
00:12:34.369 --> 00:12:36.800
We go and visit with her.

175
00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:38.421
Ms. Rogers was there.

176
00:12:38.421 --> 00:12:40.041
<v ARSENEAULT>She was at Mossville?

177
00:12:40.041 --> 00:12:41.662
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>She was at Mossville.

178
00:12:41.662 --> 00:12:45.713
Mr. [Arfay?] was there and he was one from Mossville.

179
00:12:45.713 --> 00:12:51.385
I can’t replace of any . . . the rest of them . . . No, Ms. Jones was there.

180
00:12:51.385 --> 00:12:53.410
She was our typing teacher.

181
00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:55.031
She was at Mossville.

182
00:12:55.031 --> 00:12:57.056
Then we had Mr. Martin.

183
00:12:57.056 --> 00:13:02.728
He wasn't at Mossville, but he was one of our teachers at Westlake High.

184
00:13:02.728 --> 00:13:06.779
While I'm . . . Ms. Clark was one of our white teachers.

185
00:13:06.779 --> 00:13:08.400
Very sweet English teacher.

186
00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:13.262
There was our football coach who was . . . No I'm not getting names.

187
00:13:13.262 --> 00:13:14.072
<v ARSENEAULT>From Mossville?

188
00:13:14.072 --> 00:13:14.882
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>From Westlake.

189
00:13:14.882 --> 00:13:15.692
<v ARSENEAULT>From Westlake.

190
00:13:15.692 --> 00:13:24.605
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Well Coach William . . . I think Coach William came from Mossville and I think he was at Westlake High for a little while.

191
00:13:24.605 --> 00:13:26.631
Coach William, he was there.

192
00:13:26.631 --> 00:13:29.872
There was a quite a few of them.

193
00:13:29.872 --> 00:13:36.354
I was just trying to think of the one that was real good . . . Was Coach [Hansen?],

194
00:13:36.354 --> 00:13:40.000
he was a white coach and he was there.

195
00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.026
Mr. Benson was our principal.

196
00:13:42.026 --> 00:13:44.051
It's a lot of them.

197
00:13:44.051 --> 00:13:55.395
We had a good time at the school after we were able to understand that we're going to share this school because we didn't ask to come here.

198
00:13:55.395 --> 00:14:03.497
So things got . . . But we fought a good three to four years for our rights there as an integrated school.

199
00:14:03.497 --> 00:14:11.600
It lost a lot of white students because a lot of white parents took their kids after we got there.

200
00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:13.221
They took them away.

201
00:14:13.221 --> 00:14:16.867
A lot of the white kids would not adjust.

202
00:14:16.867 --> 00:14:25.374
They just refused to share their school with us without knowing that we didn't want to come there in the beginning.

203
00:14:25.374 --> 00:14:29.831
So in Mossville we had . . . We didn't come up with whites.

204
00:14:29.831 --> 00:14:32.262
It was a predominate black community.

205
00:14:32.262 --> 00:14:42.795
It had black people that might be married or dated the white people and they came into the community, but it never was a racial community.

206
00:14:42.795 --> 00:14:44.415
We all got along.

207
00:14:44.415 --> 00:14:47.656
Everybody . . . we walked to Mossville . . . Every school . . . like,

208
00:14:47.656 --> 00:14:52.923
you didn't ride the bus unless you was over a mile from school.

209
00:14:52.923 --> 00:14:56.569
So we walked to school, to Mossville every day.

210
00:14:56.569 --> 00:14:59.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you grow up?

211
00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:00.143
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>In Mossville.

212
00:15:00.143 --> 00:15:01.286
<v ARSENEAULT>What street?

213
00:15:01.286 --> 00:15:03.000
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Old Spanish Trail.

214
00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You were on Old Spanish Trail?

215
00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:09.735
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Yeah we were on the Old Spanish Trail.

216
00:15:09.735 --> 00:15:19.309
So that . . . and the school is on the Old Spanish Trail so that made us . . . Prater Road was the cutoff for anybody who had to ride the bus.

217
00:15:19.309 --> 00:15:25.464
If you lived on Prater, or past Prater, you get a chance to ride the bus to school.

218
00:15:25.464 --> 00:15:28.883
Anyone from Prater to east of Prater . . . Is it right?

219
00:15:28.883 --> 00:15:31.618
East of Prater had to walk to school.

220
00:15:31.618 --> 00:15:35.721
So but it wasn't but a mile that we had to walk.

221
00:15:35.721 --> 00:15:40.508
But it's the getting up every morning making sure we get there on time.

222
00:15:40.508 --> 00:15:46.321
We had very beautiful teachers that treated us like . . . Ms. Paige, and Ms. [Gatten?], and Ms. Calvin.

223
00:15:46.321 --> 00:15:48.714
All these were our teachers at Mossville.

224
00:15:48.714 --> 00:15:54.527
They were . . . The school that Mossville was is that our teachers was like our mothers and fathers.

225
00:15:54.527 --> 00:16:01.365
You . . . Whatever you done at school and you got in trouble you got whipped by the teacher and your parents.

226
00:16:01.365 --> 00:16:04.785
The teacher would even come and eat with your parents.

227
00:16:04.785 --> 00:16:08.546
On lunch break they go to your parents’ house to eat.

228
00:16:08.546 --> 00:16:13.674
When they knew that your parent could cook, the parents was cooking for the teachers.

229
00:16:13.674 --> 00:16:16.068
So you didn't get away with nothing.

230
00:16:16.068 --> 00:16:19.829
Our teachers were . . . And you was going to get your education.

231
00:16:19.829 --> 00:16:28.377
You were going to do your homework too because it didn't take a teacher but one second to call your parents and tell on you.

232
00:16:28.377 --> 00:16:31.454
And you know that was the end of it.

233
00:16:31.454 --> 00:16:40.002
Then if they knew there were some parents who didn't care about their kids’ education they took control and the parents let them do it.

234
00:16:40.002 --> 00:16:42.738
Because they not . . . Not that they didn't care

235
00:16:42.738 --> 00:16:48.550
<c.p>about it, they wasn't able to help their children because some of them couldn't read or write.

236
00:16:48.550 --> 00:16:52.653
So they allowed the teacher to have full control of their children.

237
00:16:52.653 --> 00:16:54.705
It was just a family school.

238
00:16:54.705 --> 00:16:55.389
Very beautiful.

239
00:16:55.389 --> 00:16:56.756
We had awesome cooks.

240
00:16:56.756 --> 00:16:58.808
Ms. Rigmaiden was our head cook.

241
00:16:58.808 --> 00:17:00.176
Everybody was like family.

242
00:17:00.176 --> 00:17:05.304
Mossville was a place where we were brought up when we walk home from school,

243
00:17:05.304 --> 00:17:13.169
you didn't pass a house coming home from school and you saw older people sitting on the porch . . . Try passing and don't speak.

244
00:17:13.169 --> 00:17:13.852
"Good evening."

245
00:17:13.852 --> 00:17:18.639
Or they'll call and say, "Yeah so-and-so passed the house didn't even speak today.

246
00:17:18.639 --> 00:17:20.691
Them children ain't got no respect."

247
00:17:20.691 --> 00:17:23.426
But that was the best part of it.

248
00:17:23.426 --> 00:17:33.000
We enjoyed passing by the old people when we would get off the school . . . Because once we were walking that way we knew we had to speak.

249
00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:38.619
But other than that, Mossville was like a community that you felt comfortable in.

250
00:17:38.619 --> 00:17:42.633
Like when I came here it's because I took sick.

251
00:17:42.633 --> 00:17:51.062
I haven't got . . . I've been here now about six years, and I cried just about every other month in six years.

252
00:17:51.062 --> 00:17:56.682
I'm crying because I don't know nobody and that's just right across the bridge.

253
00:17:56.682 --> 00:17:58.689
But I know nobody here.

254
00:17:58.689 --> 00:18:05.512
I met my neighbors, but I can ride down the street, but it's no place like home.

255
00:18:05.512 --> 00:18:17.955
If I'm in Mossville riding around I could stop off . . .if I go to the store I’d stop off and visit somebody or . . . We grew up with all the people there.

256
00:18:17.955 --> 00:18:23.574
We knew everybody and it was just a beautiful feeling to be in Mossville.

257
00:18:23.574 --> 00:18:29.194
It's like . . . I said I wasn't going to cry, but nobody knows the pain.

258
00:18:29.194 --> 00:18:32.405
It was like a safe haven for me.

259
00:18:32.405 --> 00:18:37.221
It was . . . You can walk down the street, you can play outside.

260
00:18:37.221 --> 00:18:42.841
All night we played outside with one another at night and it was safe.

261
00:18:42.841 --> 00:18:47.256
We played house in the woods and we just got along.

262
00:18:47.256 --> 00:18:48.862
And neighbors were neighbors.

263
00:18:48.862 --> 00:18:56.488
I was just telling a lady yesterday that . . . That was my best friend's birthday and she's in Washington, D.C.

264
00:18:56.488 --> 00:19:00.903
And she said, "Well you know Peggy's coming home next week."

265
00:19:00.903 --> 00:19:01.706
And we

266
00:19:01.706 --> 00:19:06.522
<c.p>grew up together and we would like, go to each other house.

267
00:19:06.522 --> 00:19:13.747
Just you was able to walk to each other's house and go to the next door neighbor house.

268
00:19:13.747 --> 00:19:15.353
And we had fights.

269
00:19:15.353 --> 00:19:17.360
We fought with each other.

270
00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:19.768
We fell out with each other,

271
00:19:19.768 --> 00:19:29.000
but we all still went to school together and enjoyed each other because of the serenity that we had knowing each other.

272
00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:33.794
Everybody looked out for one another because Lake Charles was always against Mossville.

273
00:19:33.794 --> 00:19:41.170
When the Lake Charles boys would come to Mossville, the boys from Mossville didn't want them talking to the girls.

274
00:19:41.170 --> 00:19:42.645
Well, we was family.

275
00:19:42.645 --> 00:19:48.546
We was . . . And the older people in Mossville were people that didn't mind telling on you.

276
00:19:48.546 --> 00:19:57.397
If they caught your child doing something, or your child was doing something out of the ordinary, you going to find out about it.

277
00:19:57.397 --> 00:19:59.979
Somebody going to call and tell you.

278
00:19:59.979 --> 00:20:06.986
Even after I got married people always would say . . . And I don't have a problem with saying my nickname,

279
00:20:06.986 --> 00:20:09.567
but that's one thing that Mossville had.

280
00:20:09.567 --> 00:20:11.043
We all had nicknames.

281
00:20:11.043 --> 00:20:12.518
My nickname is [Chonchi?].

282
00:20:12.518 --> 00:20:17.312
How I got [Chonchi?], my daddy was digging a well over next door.

283
00:20:17.312 --> 00:20:22.844
He was putting a well down at one of the houses that he put there.

284
00:20:22.844 --> 00:20:27.638
And while he was digging the well that was a man named [Chonchi?].

285
00:20:27.638 --> 00:20:36.858
And he was telling [Chonchi?] to pick up something, but at the same time he was asking me to get him a glass of water.

286
00:20:36.858 --> 00:20:41.652
So he wind up calling me, "[Chonchi?], get me a glass of water."

287
00:20:41.652 --> 00:20:43.865
I said, "My name ain't [Chonchi?].

288
00:20:43.865 --> 00:20:45.340
That's [Chonchi?] over there."

289
00:20:45.340 --> 00:20:47.922
But I got named [Chonchi?] after that.

290
00:20:47.922 --> 00:20:50.872
So ever since that I was named [Chonchi?].

291
00:20:50.872 --> 00:20:54.560
But we had [Chonchi?], we had Peanut, we had Chi-Chi.

292
00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:56.404
So we had them all.

293
00:20:56.404 --> 00:20:58.986
We had names that like Crazy Man.

294
00:20:58.986 --> 00:21:04.149
We had . . . A lot of names in Mossville like one lady name was [Nig?].

295
00:21:04.149 --> 00:21:09.312
I'm not going to say it because today she don't like being called [Nig?].

296
00:21:09.312 --> 00:21:10.418
We had Batman.

297
00:21:10.418 --> 00:21:13.000
We had . . . Everybody had a nickname.

298
00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:18.265
So when we went to jail that time for picketing we gave the people our nicknames.

299
00:21:18.265 --> 00:21:23.529
When they asked the man, one of my friends, what was his name he said, "Crazy

300
00:21:23.529 --> 00:21:23.858
<c.p>Man."

301
00:21:23.858 --> 00:21:26.819
He said, "Do you know your for real name?"

302
00:21:26.819 --> 00:21:28.135
He said, "Crazy Man."

303
00:21:28.135 --> 00:21:29.781
He said, "What's your name?"

304
00:21:29.781 --> 00:21:30.768
I said, "Chonchi."

305
00:21:30.768 --> 00:21:33.400
And he asked my brother he said, "Peanut."

306
00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:41.297
So we were in trouble when my dad and them found out that we didn't give our real names, but it was so funny.

307
00:21:41.297 --> 00:21:43.929
My dad didn't even know our real names.

308
00:21:43.929 --> 00:21:51.168
It's amazing that today people are still calling me Chonchi and calling and asking me what is my real name until today.

309
00:21:51.168 --> 00:21:53.800
And I say, "My real name is Christine."

310
00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:55.116
<v ARSENEAULT>What about your dad?

311
00:21:55.116 --> 00:22:01.697
I want to hear more about like your parents and kind of what they did and what they were like.

312
00:22:01.697 --> 00:22:04.000
So he was a well digger?

313
00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:10.492
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Yeah and he . . . my dad worked . . . and I don't know what year he started at the plant.

314
00:22:10.492 --> 00:22:18.511
I don't even know what my dad before he went to the plants . . . He was working at a plant called [Citcon?].

315
00:22:18.511 --> 00:22:20.802
But he worked as a janitor.

316
00:22:20.802 --> 00:22:25.003
He raised thirteen kids on a janitor’s job and digging wells.

317
00:22:25.003 --> 00:22:30.731
That's . . . My dad was out at the plant for over thirty-five years as a janitor.

318
00:22:30.731 --> 00:22:38.750
He even plant a garden at the plant and the people was eating from the garden he planted at the plant.

319
00:22:38.750 --> 00:22:45.624
He was bringing food home not knowing because of the ignorance of not knowing how horrible chemicals were.

320
00:22:45.624 --> 00:22:47.915
He had a garden out there.

321
00:22:47.915 --> 00:22:57.843
At the same time we were eating that stuff and the people there were eating it because until today people don't realize how horrible chemicals are.

322
00:22:57.843 --> 00:22:59.753
That's where my dad worked.

323
00:22:59.753 --> 00:23:01.662
My mom was a homemaker.

324
00:23:01.662 --> 00:23:07.008
Most of the mothers in the neighborhood . . . Very few families wasn't over twelve kids.

325
00:23:07.008 --> 00:23:12.354
All of them was either twelve, or thirteen, or fourteen people in the family.

326
00:23:12.354 --> 00:23:15.409
The Franks were like ten in a family.

327
00:23:15.409 --> 00:23:18.464
The Paynes were like thirteen in the family.

328
00:23:18.464 --> 00:23:21.519
The Braxtons was like thirteen in the family.

329
00:23:21.519 --> 00:23:22.665
We were twelve.

330
00:23:22.665 --> 00:23:25.338
We were all large families in Mossville.

331
00:23:25.338 --> 00:23:27.629
So the mothers all stayed home.

332
00:23:27.629 --> 00:23:28.775
All these mothers.

333
00:23:28.775 --> 00:23:31.448
It was the Bernards that were there

334
00:23:31.448 --> 00:23:35.266
<c.p>and they were like eight or nine in a family.

335
00:23:35.266 --> 00:23:37.558
And all the mothers were homemakers.

336
00:23:37.558 --> 00:23:39.467
My mother sewed our clothes.

337
00:23:39.467 --> 00:23:42.522
She was one that . . . She did it all.

338
00:23:42.522 --> 00:23:44.813
She took care of the household.

339
00:23:44.813 --> 00:23:49.396
My dad took care and my brothers took care of the outside.

340
00:23:49.396 --> 00:23:56.651
But everybody had to learn . . . The families were like the girls can do just what the boys can do.

341
00:23:56.651 --> 00:23:57.797
We cut grass.

342
00:23:57.797 --> 00:24:00.852
We went and helped with the well digging.

343
00:24:00.852 --> 00:24:04.288
We dug stuff too and we had a garden.

344
00:24:04.288 --> 00:24:06.962
Majority of the people had a garden.

345
00:24:06.962 --> 00:24:08.871
Everything we did we plant.

346
00:24:08.871 --> 00:24:18.036
A lot of times by us being twelve in the family with one bathroom my dad made his . . . built him one in the woods.

347
00:24:18.036 --> 00:24:21.854
I told him he was the first man with a . . .

348
00:24:21.854 --> 00:24:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Man cave?

349
00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:27.329
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>His own man cave and what do they call it?

350
00:24:27.329 --> 00:24:27.762
Port-a-johns?

351
00:24:27.762 --> 00:24:39.018
He was the first one make one of those. [laughs] So it was . . . We had . . . They did a lot of like they raised hogs and chickens.

352
00:24:39.018 --> 00:24:41.616
Everything was done from the land.

353
00:24:41.616 --> 00:24:50.274
So God is able to make us understand how Mossville was . . . My sisters and brothers that are now gone like,

354
00:24:50.274 --> 00:24:55.902
"Girl I never know you . . ." I was the last one to leave, too.

355
00:24:55.902 --> 00:24:57.634
Everybody else moved away,

356
00:24:57.634 --> 00:25:07.159
and I just moved away six years ago from Mossville and I never . . . Moved here but my home is still in Mossville.

357
00:25:07.159 --> 00:25:13.652
We been in a fight for the plants ever since the first one got there.

358
00:25:13.652 --> 00:25:19.713
My brothers and them couldn't understand why the plants were moving in on us.

359
00:25:19.713 --> 00:25:24.043
They had been fighting ever since I was a child.

360
00:25:24.043 --> 00:25:31.835
When I got old enough I began to fight with them for . . . Is these plants causing the cancer,

361
00:25:31.835 --> 00:25:34.433
and asthma, and the kidney failure?

362
00:25:34.433 --> 00:25:40.061
What was . . . Then of course we all realized that they were bringing jobs.

363
00:25:40.061 --> 00:25:50.884
But nobody was telling us about how these people . . . but they all . . . Every time we found out about industry it was always an undercover thing.

364
00:25:50.884 --> 00:25:59.976
They went and took some older person off aside and offered them some money for their land and the older people

365
00:25:59.976 --> 00:26:02.573
<c.p>didn't understand what they were doing.

366
00:26:02.573 --> 00:26:07.768
And the people . . . God forgive me, that's just what white folks did.

367
00:26:07.768 --> 00:26:19.024
If they take a black person with . . . Uneducated didn't know nothing about business and they took them for granted on their ignorance about buying out stuff.

368
00:26:19.024 --> 00:26:26.817
So a lot of our older people sold land to the industries not understanding what they were doing.

369
00:26:26.817 --> 00:26:32.445
All they know is that back then the money was hard to get.

370
00:26:32.445 --> 00:26:40.671
And somebody come along and offer you some money that you had they was quick to sell the property.

371
00:26:40.671 --> 00:26:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the first plant that came into Mossville?

372
00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:49.956
<v DELMA BENNETT>I really couldn't say which one was the first one.

373
00:26:49.956 --> 00:26:54.912
I would imagine it would have been Olin or Conoco.

374
00:26:54.912 --> 00:27:03.336
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>To be in Mossville because there were . . . You would take . . . Citcon and Citco are older, older plants.

375
00:27:03.336 --> 00:27:08.292
They were there, but they wasn't . . . They were surrounding Mossville.

376
00:27:08.292 --> 00:27:15.726
But the one that come into Mossville, like he said, it'd been Conoco or Olin.

377
00:27:15.726 --> 00:27:19.690
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did people work before the plants came?

378
00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:23.655
Where were some places like your grandparents worked?

379
00:27:23.655 --> 00:27:26.628
<v DELMA BENNETT>Most of them worked in homes.

380
00:27:26.628 --> 00:27:34.062
Most of them were maids, and grass cutters, and lumber companies, and places like that.

381
00:27:34.062 --> 00:27:35.549
Most of them.

382
00:27:35.549 --> 00:27:37.035
When the plant . . .

383
00:27:37.035 --> 00:27:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there a lumber company in Mossville?

384
00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:43.969
<v DELMA BENNETT>No they didn't have one in Mossville.

385
00:27:43.969 --> 00:27:51.179
It was big lumber company they had was over here in Lake Charles called [Cushioner?] Lumber Company.

386
00:27:51.179 --> 00:27:54.147
Then they had other lumber companies, too.

387
00:27:54.147 --> 00:28:02.205
But there weren't really a lot of major jobs for any blacks in this community until the plant came.

388
00:28:02.205 --> 00:28:09.415
But see when the plant came in, I guess shortly after the industrial revolution here in Mossville,

389
00:28:09.415 --> 00:28:14.929
but see the city of Mossville was there before the plants got there.

390
00:28:14.929 --> 00:28:15.777
The community.

391
00:28:15.777 --> 00:28:25.531
So it wasn't really nothing for them to be able to move in because they was . . . I mean we didn't have any protest,

392
00:28:25.531 --> 00:28:31.045
riots, anything like that to really keep anybody from doing anything like that.

393
00:28:31.045 --> 00:28:40.799
When they moved in, a lot of people was kind of glad because of the fact that it created a new job market.

394
00:28:40.799 --> 00:28:44.616
And in creating a job market, they started growing.

395
00:28:44.616 --> 00:28:46.312
The community started growing.

396
00:28:46.312 --> 00:28:52.674
Some of the people in Mossville was able to get these jobs at the plants,

397
00:28:52.674 --> 00:28:57.763
but it was never on the scale of operator, or skilled labor.

398
00:28:57.763 --> 00:29:01.156
It was always janitor jobs and like that.

399
00:29:01.156 --> 00:29:16.000
Then the first ones to really get in would be the light skinned blacks before the dark skinned blacks would get in because they still had the racial thing going on at that time.

400
00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:25.163
When they came in, no one took into consideration what would happen by them coming in other than from the perspective of jobs.

401
00:29:25.163 --> 00:29:30.343
Not realizing there would be pollution that would be involved with these plants,

402
00:29:30.343 --> 00:29:36.319
and that it would also affect your water and your air and what have you.

403
00:29:36.319 --> 00:29:41.498
So it was years before they really realized that these things were happening.

404
00:29:41.498 --> 00:29:47.474
At first it wasn't really nothing that you could complain about, because everything was new.

405
00:29:47.474 --> 00:29:50.263
Then later on people started getting sick.

406
00:29:50.263 --> 00:29:55.442
They couldn't understand why they were getting sick like they were getting sick.

407
00:29:55.442 --> 00:30:00.622
Then there was no regulations that was really governing the plants at that

408
00:30:00.622 --> 00:30:01.020
<c.p>time.

409
00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:06.996
I don't even think EPA [Environmental Protection Agency] was even in existence during that time.

410
00:30:06.996 --> 00:30:13.769
So whatever they would produce . . . Everything that would come from the plant would go into the waters.

411
00:30:13.769 --> 00:30:24.526
All the waste and stuff would go into the water and after a while that started affecting the whole community with the way the waste was going,

412
00:30:24.526 --> 00:30:26.518
and the sickness started occurring.

413
00:30:26.518 --> 00:30:34.088
We got involved in it back in the 70s is when we actually got involved in what was happening.

414
00:30:34.088 --> 00:30:42.853
There was a lady by the name of Ms. [Marleen?] Ross that opened my eyes to what was happening in our community.

415
00:30:42.853 --> 00:30:51.219
Then another girl by the name of Debra Sullivan who really educated me on the fact that these things was happening.

416
00:30:51.219 --> 00:30:56.000
Then this lady that put out this documentary on Blue Vinyl . . .

417
00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:57.909
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know who Debra Ramirez is?

418
00:30:57.909 --> 00:30:58.182
<v DELMA BENNETT>Huh?

419
00:30:58.182 --> 00:30:59.273
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>That's the same person.

420
00:30:59.273 --> 00:30:59.818
<v ARSENEAULT>Same person?

421
00:30:59.818 --> 00:31:00.636
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>[agrees] Debra Sullivan.

422
00:31:00.636 --> 00:31:00.909
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

423
00:31:00.909 --> 00:31:02.000
The same person.

424
00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:05.523
<v DELMA BENNETT>She . . . I don't know if you've interviewed her or not.

425
00:31:05.523 --> 00:31:06.227
<v ARSENEAULT>Not yet.

426
00:31:06.227 --> 00:31:06.932
<v DELMA BENNETT>Oh man.

427
00:31:06.932 --> 00:31:08.341
You would enjoy her.

428
00:31:08.341 --> 00:31:12.568
<v ARSENEAULT>When did you first become aware that there was pollution in Mossville?

429
00:31:12.568 --> 00:31:17.852
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Well ours started way before, like he said [Marleen?] Ross, we was aware of it.

430
00:31:17.852 --> 00:31:22.784
When the first called shelter in place . . . When we were children at the school.

431
00:31:22.784 --> 00:31:28.068
We never understood what shelter in place . . . They made them cut the air conditioners off,

432
00:31:28.068 --> 00:31:35.466
close all the windows at school because there was some kind of leak or an emission for something that had happened.

433
00:31:35.466 --> 00:31:38.636
But being young they never told us about it.

434
00:31:38.636 --> 00:31:45.682
As we got older we realized that a lot of this stuff was going on undercover and people knew it,

435
00:31:45.682 --> 00:31:52.727
but our older people was . . . They were like not afraid of industries, but they didn't want to lose their jobs.

436
00:31:52.727 --> 00:31:54.136
That's what happens now.

437
00:31:54.136 --> 00:31:58.011
A lot of them still got that same thing going on.

438
00:31:58.011 --> 00:32:02.943
They don't want to say much because they don't want to lose they job.

439
00:32:02.943 --> 00:32:08.932
Well it's just sad that the world is built on the dollar and not on our health.

440
00:32:08.932 --> 00:32:10.341
That's what's happening now.

441
00:32:10.341 --> 00:32:14.920
It was way back in . . . like I said I was born in '53.

442
00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:18.091
I say this was started maybe in the 60s.

443
00:32:18.091 --> 00:32:30.068
That's how long it . . . They people been fighting and putting up with the plants moving in and all the chemicals because we were still at Mossville School and had to do shelter in place.

444
00:32:30.068 --> 00:32:32.534
Really, shelter in place wasn't doing anything.

445
00:32:32.534 --> 00:32:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How did they let you know?

446
00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:38.233
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>It was like a school broadcast over the news.

447
00:32:38.233 --> 00:32:45.417
The school board would always get it, or the alarm would go off and they would look at the news,

448
00:32:45.417 --> 00:32:50.806
or had something on and it would tell them it was a shelter in place.

449
00:32:50.806 --> 00:32:55.476
You can hear it like a big microphone over the . . . from the plants.

450
00:32:55.476 --> 00:32:58.350
They would say, "Alarm" or "Shelter in place."

451
00:32:58.350 --> 00:33:00.146
The adults would know it.

452
00:33:00.146 --> 00:33:05.175
So then asked everybody to close the windows and cut the air conditionings off.

453
00:33:05.175 --> 00:33:11.641
Then they would send out a call, but people would do this and some people didn't even know.

454
00:33:11.641 --> 00:33:20.981
Some people didn't even get the alarm or get the message, but if you was in school you got it because it was the school board.

455
00:33:20.981 --> 00:33:28.165
They had to let them know, but some of the homes in Mossville, they didn't know what was going on.

456
00:33:28.165 --> 00:33:35.709
The old people didn't know what that was when that alarm would go off because they never did tell us anything.

457
00:33:35.709 --> 00:33:41.456
They'd do just what as we got older and found out they were doing everything undercover.

458
00:33:41.456 --> 00:33:46.126
They never just . . . They called themselves neighbors, but they never was our neighbor.

459
00:33:46.126 --> 00:33:50.796
They was our enemy all the time, but they was supplying good jobs.

460
00:33:50.796 --> 00:33:55.466
But at the same time they was killing our people, and still is.

461
00:33:55.466 --> 00:34:07.320
<v DELMA BENNETT>The smells that took place in the community was something that was considered normal because of the fact that they just assumed that because the plant was there and the smells was coming,

462
00:34:07.320 --> 00:34:10.553
that it was just something coming from the plants.

463
00:34:10.553 --> 00:34:12.709
Then when it would get unbearable,

464
00:34:12.709 --> 00:34:23.845
then they would actually have . . . The kids would be going to the playground playing and the smell just got so bad they would just have the kids move into the school.

465
00:34:23.845 --> 00:34:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What did it smell like?

466
00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:33.698
<v DELMA BENNETT>Well they have an old . . . Conoco put out a smell that was like a rotten egg smell.

467
00:34:33.698 --> 00:34:34.604
<v ARSENEAULT>So sulfur?

468
00:34:34.604 --> 00:34:38.679
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah it was like a rotten egg smell and . . .

469
00:34:38.679 --> 00:34:39.132
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Chlorine.

470
00:34:39.132 --> 00:34:41.849
<v DELMA BENNETT>Olin smell was an ammonia smell.

471
00:34:41.849 --> 00:34:49.547
Each of them had their own different smells that came from their plants because they produce different . . .

472
00:34:49.547 --> 00:34:50.000
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Chemicals.

473
00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:50.453
<v DELMA BENNETT>Chemicals.

474
00:34:50.453 --> 00:34:54.981
Sometime it would go all the way to Lake Charles.

475
00:34:54.981 --> 00:35:02.226
It just depended on how the wind would blow would determine who would get to smell.

476
00:35:02.226 --> 00:35:14.000
And see I weren't really aware of anything pertaining to the plants until they had the big explosion at Cities Service back in the 60s.

477
00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:14.412
<v ARSENEAULT>Sixties?

478
00:35:14.412 --> 00:35:14.824
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah.

479
00:35:14.824 --> 00:35:28.824
There was a big explosion that took place and it rocked not only Mossville . . . I can imagine how devastating they were behind the explosion that took place because it broke windows in Lake Charles,

480
00:35:28.824 --> 00:35:32.941
and that's almost ten miles from where Cities Service was.

481
00:35:32.941 --> 00:35:36.235
We really didn't know what was going on.

482
00:35:36.235 --> 00:35:54.353
But after the explosion it seemed like a blessing to us because a lot of the young people were able to go get jobs and stuff over at the plant not realizing that they were going into chemicals that they weren't really protected from.

483
00:35:54.353 --> 00:35:59.294
They might have given us respirators and stuff like that to wear,

484
00:35:59.294 --> 00:36:08.765
but we weren't aware that we were being endangered by any particular type of chemical that could have been cancer causing or asbestos.

485
00:36:08.765 --> 00:36:21.529
Of course asbestos was in it because most of the plants was insulated by the asbestos and stuff and they never really said that asbestos caused cancer and what have you.

486
00:36:21.529 --> 00:36:25.235
It was just . . . Everything was just a free fall.

487
00:36:25.235 --> 00:36:31.000
Just do what you want to do as long as you making money.

488
00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:38.573
Then when we got involved in it, then we started realizing that we had been placed . . . Not placed,

489
00:36:38.573 --> 00:36:46.568
but they had came in upon Mossville the way they did with no respect involved in it at all.

490
00:36:46.568 --> 00:36:49.513
Then Westlake, they benefited from the plants.

491
00:36:49.513 --> 00:36:58.769
They got . . . They didn't really get a lot of tax revenue because I heard the parish was getting all the tax revenue.

492
00:36:58.769 --> 00:37:06.764
But they were getting a lot of blessings from the plant because the plant was right upon them also,

493
00:37:06.764 --> 00:37:13.496
and they helped them be able to develop Westlake to be the way it is today.

494
00:37:13.496 --> 00:37:21.911
So they won't hardly complain against the plant because of the fact that the money flow that goes into Westlake.

495
00:37:21.911 --> 00:37:31.588
Even the mayor himself would say that they can actually see the smoke and stuff that was coming from the plants and stuff.

496
00:37:31.588 --> 00:37:41.686
And they would just look at it like a normal occurrence that took place, not realizing that it was causing effects of the body.

497
00:37:41.686 --> 00:37:46.735
So these are the things that’s been taking place for many years.

498
00:37:46.735 --> 00:37:52.205
They utilize that area more so because the minority was in that area.

499
00:37:52.205 --> 00:37:53.467
They had free

500
00:37:53.467 --> 00:37:55.150
<c.p>access to the waterway.

501
00:37:55.150 --> 00:37:59.778
You see, and you can import and export goods very easily.

502
00:37:59.778 --> 00:38:11.559
So they did a lot of dredging and stuff to the lake in order to get big ships in to take oil and all that kind of stuff.

503
00:38:11.559 --> 00:38:18.712
Sometimes they would waste oil in the water and stuff and nobody wouldn't say anything about it.

504
00:38:18.712 --> 00:38:20.395
They just wasted oil.

505
00:38:20.395 --> 00:38:31.334
Then the areas that the other plants that had their release areas in on the plant would have big rains and all that kind of stuff.

506
00:38:31.334 --> 00:38:37.646
The stuff would overflow and go into Bayou D'Inde and all that kind of stuff.

507
00:38:37.646 --> 00:38:39.749
It just was a norm.

508
00:38:39.749 --> 00:38:49.847
People would go and eat the crabs and the crawfishes and the fish and all that stuff that was in the waters and stuff.

509
00:38:49.847 --> 00:38:51.951
Didn't think nothing of it.

510
00:38:51.951 --> 00:38:52.793
<v ARSENEAULT>Not knowing.

511
00:38:52.793 --> 00:38:53.213
<v DELMA BENNETT>Huh?

512
00:38:53.213 --> 00:38:54.055
<v ARSENEAULT>Not knowing.

513
00:38:54.055 --> 00:38:54.896
<v DELMA BENNETT>No. No.

514
00:38:54.896 --> 00:38:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was going on.

515
00:38:57.000 --> 00:38:58.179
<v DELMA BENNETT>They didn't know.

516
00:38:58.179 --> 00:39:08.393
I mean they just . . . It was just . . . I mean the crabs can . . . the crabs and the catfish and the different scavengers . . . Shrimps and all that stuff.

517
00:39:08.393 --> 00:39:15.464
It was a blessing to them because their job was to more or less keep the waterway clean.

518
00:39:15.464 --> 00:39:17.429
To us it was a . . .

519
00:39:17.429 --> 00:39:17.821
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Delicacy.

520
00:39:17.821 --> 00:39:19.786
<v DELMA BENNETT>Can't get the word out . . .

521
00:39:19.786 --> 00:39:20.179
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Delicacy.

522
00:39:20.179 --> 00:39:20.964
<v DELMA BENNETT>[laughs] Delicacy.

523
00:39:20.964 --> 00:39:22.536
They just enjoyed it.

524
00:39:22.536 --> 00:39:28.821
Because I remember when I was small here in Mossville I would go in Lake Charles.

525
00:39:28.821 --> 00:39:34.714
I would go in the ditch and get crawfish out of the ditch and stuff.

526
00:39:34.714 --> 00:39:37.857
Take it home and boil them and stuff.

527
00:39:37.857 --> 00:39:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So you grew up in Lake Charles?

528
00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:43.194
<v DELMA BENNETT>I grew up in Lake Charles.

529
00:39:43.194 --> 00:39:46.120
<v ARSENEAULT>What made your family move here from California?

530
00:39:46.120 --> 00:39:48.680
<v DELMA BENNETT>Well my family never moved from California.

531
00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:53.800
My mom just sent me to live with my grandmother here in Lake Charles.

532
00:39:53.800 --> 00:40:05.869
I stayed with my grandmother until I was . . . Well off and on because I went back to my mom a couple of . . . One time and then I came back and finished high school.

533
00:40:05.869 --> 00:40:11.720
Then I went back to Lake Charles, I mean San Francisco, and went to college there.

534
00:40:11.720 --> 00:40:14.280
Then I went to the army there.

535
00:40:14.280 --> 00:40:16.840
Then I went to trade school there.

536
00:40:16.840 --> 00:40:26.714
Then I just . . . I met my wife and I came back to . . . When I came back I met my wife and I've been here ever since then.

537
00:40:26.714 --> 00:40:28.543
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all meet in Mossville?

538
00:40:28.543 --> 00:40:31.834
<v DELMA BENNETT>No we met at the beach in Lake Charles.

539
00:40:31.834 --> 00:40:36.954
There was a segregated beach that we . . . No, it wasn't segregated at the time,

540
00:40:36.954 --> 00:40:45.000
but we were at that beach in Mossville at the time . . . I mean Lake Charles at the time when we met.

541
00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:46.103
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>You know what?

542
00:40:46.103 --> 00:40:54.924
The main thing that I wanted them to understand when they segregation started, we had to travel through industries to get to Westlake High.

543
00:40:54.924 --> 00:41:04.114
We never wanted that route because every morning we had to smell that smell on our way to school because that was . . . I don't know,

544
00:41:04.114 --> 00:41:08.524
it didn't have to be the only way we could get there,

545
00:41:08.524 --> 00:41:14.038
but we had to pass through all the industries every morning to get to school.

546
00:41:14.038 --> 00:41:19.919
And on that bus we had to keep the windows closed because we couldn't hardly breathe.

547
00:41:19.919 --> 00:41:26.535
We had to deal with this every day to go to high school; to get to Westlake High.

548
00:41:26.535 --> 00:41:33.886
We had to pass there, and the kids are still passing through those industries to get to school every day.

549
00:41:33.886 --> 00:41:50.059
No one ever thought about the rights of us as African American children to get a new route to send the kids because there was no new route because every route you took you had to pass through industries to get us to school.

550
00:41:50.059 --> 00:41:51.897
Nobody ever thought about that.

551
00:41:51.897 --> 00:41:53.000
Nobody cared.

552
00:41:53.000 --> 00:41:56.759
So what we have gone through we letting them know.

553
00:41:56.759 --> 00:42:03.526
People say that we're asking for too much money, but how can you pay somebody for they life?

554
00:42:03.526 --> 00:42:08.414
You can’t pay for our heritage and you can't pay for our health.

555
00:42:08.414 --> 00:42:12.173
But yet they’re [benefiting?] from our heritage and our health.

556
00:42:12.173 --> 00:42:15.932
Not really to our health, but they're . . . from out land.

557
00:42:15.932 --> 00:42:17.436
They're profiting from it.

558
00:42:17.436 --> 00:42:24.203
But yet the little money we ask for they say they can't afford to give it to us.

559
00:42:24.203 --> 00:42:26.083
But yet they uprooting us.

560
00:42:26.083 --> 00:42:29.842
If we stay in Mossville who going to move it?

561
00:42:29.842 --> 00:42:32.098
Who going to buy our land?

562
00:42:32.098 --> 00:42:33.602
Who going . . . How we

563
00:42:33.602 --> 00:42:36.233
<c.p>going to be able to sell it?

564
00:42:36.233 --> 00:42:41.120
Because the whole area southwest of the bridge is going to be industries.

565
00:42:41.120 --> 00:42:43.000
Ain't got much left.

566
00:42:43.000 --> 00:42:49.413
It's already . . . It's planned already that they going to go all the way up to the DeQuincy Highway with this.

567
00:42:49.413 --> 00:42:56.787
Really all the way to Texas because that's where they're trying to join in with Texas because the pipeline is coming from Texas.

568
00:42:56.787 --> 00:43:02.559
So they going to meet up the pipelines and the pipelines in Louisiana are going to meet up,

569
00:43:02.559 --> 00:43:05.444
and this whole southwest is going to be industry.

570
00:43:05.444 --> 00:43:11.537
The only difference the African American community was the first one that they going to take advantage of first.

571
00:43:11.537 --> 00:43:17.949
Now when they get ready to deal with Sulphur, and Maplewood, and Westlake, ain't going to be no problems then.

572
00:43:17.949 --> 00:43:23.400
They going to have all the money they need then because my grandfather say it's oil there.

573
00:43:23.400 --> 00:43:32.698
That's rich land in Mossville and they know it because how can a company come and pay nine million dollars for a school and tear it down next week?

574
00:43:32.698 --> 00:43:34.622
They wasn't paying for that school.

575
00:43:34.622 --> 00:43:36.546
They was paying for that land.

576
00:43:36.546 --> 00:43:39.752
It's just what they did the people in Bel Air.

577
00:43:39.752 --> 00:43:47.127
They gave the people in . . . They bought the land and they didn't give them no mineral rights for ten years they gave it,

578
00:43:47.127 --> 00:43:50.654
but they didn't start doing nothing until after the ten years.

579
00:43:50.654 --> 00:44:00.594
That's the same thing they want to do with . . . they won't even allow us to get . . . They want to say all you can do is get mineral rights for ten years.

580
00:44:00.594 --> 00:44:01.235
That's sad.

581
00:44:01.235 --> 00:44:06.044
Why our mineral rights can't be our mineral rights just like there's going to be?

582
00:44:06.044 --> 00:44:06.686
For life.

583
00:44:06.686 --> 00:44:11.175
Whatever you get there, whatever you profit from, we should profit from it, too.

584
00:44:11.175 --> 00:44:12.778
But they won't do it.

585
00:44:12.778 --> 00:44:17.587
But I guarantee you later on in life when they getting ready to buy Maplewood,

586
00:44:17.587 --> 00:44:22.076
and Sulphur, and Westlake they going to get a better deal with this poor . . .

587
00:44:22.076 --> 00:44:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Because it's a white community?

588
00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:25.608
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>They all white communities.

589
00:44:25.608 --> 00:44:29.628
But they always haul off the poor, little blacks first.

590
00:44:29.628 --> 00:44:37.267
Making us look like we're stupid and ignorant, but we're standing still and nobody won't help us to fight.

591
00:44:37.267 --> 00:44:39.679
Nobody won't help us to fight.

592
00:44:39.679 --> 00:44:46.111
They just . . . Like we found out we're the third African heritage community that's being bought out.

593
00:44:46.111 --> 00:44:49.730
We're the third one now because it was [Diamond?].

594
00:44:49.730 --> 00:44:51.740
<v DELMA BENNETT>It was a Diamond community,

595
00:44:51.740 --> 00:45:00.182
but the thing was when Sasol moved in they moved in on . . . It wasn't just the blacks that they moved in.

596
00:45:00.182 --> 00:45:08.625
They moved in on the white community that was in the area that they needed to set up their main plants.

597
00:45:08.625 --> 00:45:11.439
That area was bought out with very,

598
00:45:11.439 --> 00:45:23.902
very few resistance because the people in that community was given enough money where they had no reason to complain about at all because Sasol said that they needed that land.

599
00:45:23.902 --> 00:45:29.530
So they gave them practically exactly what they thought would be fair to them.

600
00:45:29.530 --> 00:45:42.797
So by . . . They even had a member of the official board of Westlake that was in that area and I know that they offered them at least a few million for their property.

601
00:45:42.797 --> 00:45:54.858
I know they did that [to call the bankings?] but they refused to go even with the millions that they offered them because they wanted a certain amount of millions.

602
00:45:54.858 --> 00:45:59.280
They didn't just want the few millions that they give them.

603
00:45:59.280 --> 00:46:02.095
They wanted a certain amount of millions.

604
00:46:02.095 --> 00:46:18.176
Not only that, there was a road that lead from their community through our community to get to Sulphur and they kept that road open just for them to be able to go through our community to get to Sulphur.

605
00:46:18.176 --> 00:46:23.000
That's the kind of stuff they did for the white community.

606
00:46:23.000 --> 00:46:27.095
So they said with our community that they didn't need our community.

607
00:46:27.095 --> 00:46:32.214
They said . . . We don't need our community, but our community was in the buffer zone.

608
00:46:32.214 --> 00:46:33.579
I don't know if

609
00:46:33.579 --> 00:46:38.357
<c.p>you know what buffer zone means, but our community was in the buffer zone.

610
00:46:38.357 --> 00:46:45.865
So they said they what they would do, they would buy our community, but they would buy it on a volunteer base.

611
00:46:45.865 --> 00:46:54.738
So they say said if we buy on a volunteer base, the way that we would do it, we'd give each home owner a 100,000 dollars.

612
00:46:54.738 --> 00:47:06.000
Then we will appraise their home and we'll give them sixty percent of whatever we appraise their home at and that would be the deal if you want to sell your home.

613
00:47:06.000 --> 00:47:15.502
But here you are coming into a community and you starting off from the start, "I'm going to cut your lifestyle in half."

614
00:47:15.502 --> 00:47:23.351
They automatically telling you however you living in this community your community . . . your lifestyle will be cut in half.

615
00:47:23.351 --> 00:47:27.896
So with my home, I felt like I wanted to leave.

616
00:47:27.896 --> 00:47:34.919
I wanted to leave because of the fact that even here in Lake Charles we're not safe.

617
00:47:34.919 --> 00:47:46.073
Actually nowhere in America or in the world that we're actually safe as long as there's the fossil fuels that's being used for our means of progress.

618
00:47:46.073 --> 00:47:48.139
So I felt like this.

619
00:47:48.139 --> 00:47:55.988
In order for me to be able to leave Mossville, I want to be able to leave Mossville whole.

620
00:47:55.988 --> 00:48:03.425
I don't want to have to go through anything that I wasn't going through where I am now.

621
00:48:03.425 --> 00:48:07.969
So I was fortunate enough to leave Mossville on my own.

622
00:48:07.969 --> 00:48:23.255
The only reason why I left Mossville on my own is because my wife had went into the hospital and a code blue was put on her and they haven't told her today what caused her illness.

623
00:48:23.255 --> 00:48:33.583
She even tried to get a toxicologist to examine her and they refused to examine her when they found out that she was from Mossville.

624
00:48:33.583 --> 00:48:42.259
She haven't been examined to find out what her sickness was and they had gave her every sickness under the sun.

625
00:48:42.259 --> 00:48:49.695
Every sickness under the sun they gave . . . they even tried to give her sex . . . What you call it?

626
00:48:49.695 --> 00:48:50.108
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Disease.

627
00:48:50.108 --> 00:48:51.347
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh an STD?

628
00:48:51.347 --> 00:48:53.000
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Yeah an STD.

629
