836
01:00:31.941 --> 01:00:41.306
and a lot of their land got donated just because the Mosses or the Rigmaiden's donated the land for us to have our first school.

837
01:00:41.306 --> 01:00:48.049
So a lot of the stuff that was done, people donated land, and that's the big argument now.

838
01:00:48.049 --> 01:00:48.798
What happens?

839
01:00:48.798 --> 01:00:51.420
The land wasn't sold, it was donated.

840
01:00:51.420 --> 01:00:54.417
Just like the recreation and the swimming pool.

841
01:00:54.417 --> 01:01:01.909
That was land that was donated to put that there for the young people to have something in the community.

842
01:01:01.909 --> 01:01:04.906
So if it was donated it wasn't sold.

843
01:01:04.906 --> 01:01:06.029
It was donated.

844
01:01:06.029 --> 01:01:15.020
So they had to go through all that to find out how to get rid of this land and that's why it's still here.

845
01:01:15.020 --> 01:01:21.013
So it had nothing to do with, oh one had this education, or that one had . . .

846
01:01:21.013 --> 01:01:22.137
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yes it did.

847
01:01:22.137 --> 01:01:24.384
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Some of it played a role.

848
01:01:24.384 --> 01:01:27.756
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yes it did because Montgomery was a business man.

849
01:01:27.756 --> 01:01:29.629
He had a night club.

850
01:01:29.629 --> 01:01:31.876
A big night club in Mossville.

851
01:01:31.876 --> 01:01:33.000
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Oh yeah.

852
01:01:33.000 --> 01:01:33.942
<v DELMA BENNETT>The Paradise Club?

853
01:01:33.942 --> 01:01:34.256
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Paradise.

854
01:01:34.256 --> 01:01:38.340
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah and he brought in entertainers from all over the country to Mossville.

855
01:01:38.340 --> 01:01:41.481
I mean it didn't make him a spokesman or nothing,

856
01:01:41.481 --> 01:01:49.648
but it put him in a position where if somebody came they would go to him because he had a connection with a lot of people.

857
01:01:49.648 --> 01:01:55.617
Like I said the Rigmaidens, because of the fact that they were some of the founding fathers, they had . . .

858
01:01:55.617 --> 01:01:55.931
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Mosses.

859
01:01:55.931 --> 01:01:57.501
<v DELMA BENNETT>And the Mosses and stuff.

860
01:01:57.501 --> 01:01:57.816
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Praters.

861
01:01:57.816 --> 01:02:02.527
<v DELMA BENNETT>They were able to send their kids to school and all this kind of stuff.

862
01:02:02.527 --> 01:02:07.239
Got college educations and stuff like that, but they were never in the political front.

863
01:02:07.239 --> 01:02:12.265
To the point that we could do this, or we can do that for our community.

864
01:02:12.265 --> 01:02:22.945
We didn't know . . . I don't think if we really did know they could have done these things for their community because they didn't really have any . . . nobody from outside would come in and say,

865
01:02:22.945 --> 01:02:25.458
"Well maybe you guys ought to do this."

866
01:02:25.458 --> 01:02:32.369
First time I ever heard about being incorporated was by a guy by the name of Mark [Defrin?] had mentioned to me,

867
01:02:32.369 --> 01:02:34.254
"Why y'all have never been incorporated?"

868
01:02:34.254 --> 01:02:42.107
See if we'd been incorporated, we'd have a tax base where we could have done a whole lot of different things than what we've done.

869
01:02:42.107 --> 01:02:46.818
They could have really grew at that time, but we stayed stagnated because of that.

870
01:02:46.818 --> 01:02:51.530
Looking for our masters to take care of us in the position that we're in.

871
01:02:51.530 --> 01:02:55.928
Even though they say, "If you don't have a political clout you just there."

872
01:02:55.928 --> 01:03:03.781
But with a political clout you have something that you can say, "Well you can do this, or can do that, or can't do this,

873
01:03:03.781 --> 01:03:14.461
or you can’t do that because of the fact that I have someone that goes to these different meetings with the different mayor and stuff and say the things that they have to say."

874
01:03:14.461 --> 01:03:16.032
But they didn't have that.

875
01:03:16.032 --> 01:03:22.000
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>I think that's why a lot of the African American communities get bought out because they're not incorporated.

876
01:03:22.000 --> 01:03:23.938
<v DELMA BENNETT>That's not why they get bought out.

877
01:03:23.938 --> 01:03:26.708
The reason why they get bought out because they are . . .

878
01:03:26.708 --> 01:03:33.077
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>I mean they don't have nobody else to fight for them because like right now we don't have nobody to fight for us.

879
01:03:33.077 --> 01:03:33.354
Nobody.

880
01:03:33.354 --> 01:03:33.908
So I’m . . .

881
01:03:33.908 --> 01:03:35.846
<v DELMA BENNETT>That's not why they get bought out.

882
01:03:35.846 --> 01:03:40.000
They get bought out because they was put in a land that they didn't want.

883
01:03:40.000 --> 01:03:43.323
The people . . . The master didn't want that land in the first place.

884
01:03:43.323 --> 01:03:55.508
And we went there and we developed it and then later on they found out that that was a valuable piece of land because the industrial . . . Because there was no such thing as the industrial revolution until around the thirties or something I think.

885
01:03:55.508 --> 01:03:58.000
I don't know exactly when the industrial revolution started,

886
01:03:58.000 --> 01:04:02.985
but when the industrial revolution started they had to be able to put these plants in certain areas.

887
01:04:02.985 --> 01:04:09.908
Most of the areas that they had to put it in where they had access to the highway, or they had access to the waterway.

888
01:04:09.908 --> 01:04:15.446
Most of the black communities was placed in those areas because nobody wanted to be confronted with them snakes and . . .

889
01:04:15.446 --> 01:04:16.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

890
01:04:16.000 --> 01:04:17.599
<v DELMA BENNETT>Alligators and all that stuff.

891
01:04:17.599 --> 01:04:22.716
Give it them for a little or nothing and let them stay there and develop it.

892
01:04:22.716 --> 01:04:23.676
And they did.

893
01:04:23.676 --> 01:04:25.595
Now later on it becomes valuable.

894
01:04:25.595 --> 01:04:30.392
So this is really my land anyway even though the land was for the Indians.

895
01:04:30.392 --> 01:04:31.991
This is my land anyway.

896
01:04:31.991 --> 01:04:36.468
I ought to be able to get it how I want to get it.

897
01:04:36.468 --> 01:04:41.586
They can't come tell me how to get my land back that I gave to them.

898
01:04:41.586 --> 01:04:44.144
So this is the dilemma we're in now.

899
01:04:44.144 --> 01:04:50.860
They want their land back and they going to give it to us the way they want us to have it.

900
01:04:50.860 --> 01:04:52.779
That's what we’re confronted with now.

901
01:04:52.779 --> 01:04:59.176
We got a meeting Friday where we have to go to discuss . . . Where they discuss why they can do this,

902
01:04:59.176 --> 01:05:05.572
or why they can do that, and why they not going to this, or why they not going to do

903
01:05:05.572 --> 01:05:05.892
<c.p>that.

904
01:05:05.892 --> 01:05:11.009
They still say, “If you don't want to sell don't sell because it's a volunteer buyout.”

905
01:05:11.009 --> 01:05:14.847
I'm not selling mines unless I get what I want for mines.

906
01:05:14.847 --> 01:05:17.725
All I want to do is to leave whole.

907
01:05:17.725 --> 01:05:25.401
I don't want to owe nobody nothing and I still want my 100,000 dollars clear along with the same amount that I have there.

908
01:05:25.401 --> 01:05:27.000
That's what I want.

909
01:05:27.000 --> 01:05:32.409
<v ARSENEAULT>So that first spill back in '94, what do y'all remember about that?

910
01:05:32.409 --> 01:05:37.818
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah it did . . . What happened with that was what was a big farce.

911
01:05:37.818 --> 01:05:41.978
The spill that took place was going on for years.

912
01:05:41.978 --> 01:05:43.642
<v ARSENEAULT>And it was Vista?

913
01:05:43.642 --> 01:05:44.058
<v DELMA BENNETT>Vista.

914
01:05:44.058 --> 01:05:47.387
But it had been going on for years.

915
01:05:47.387 --> 01:05:53.212
And they knew . . . I mean I'm speculating now, or going on a conspiracy theory.

916
01:05:53.212 --> 01:05:56.124
They knew that the spill was there.

917
01:05:56.124 --> 01:06:05.277
If they didn't know that the spill was there, why would they go and build a whole sewage project in that area?

918
01:06:05.277 --> 01:06:08.190
They build something we didn't even have.

919
01:06:08.190 --> 01:06:09.854
What you call it?

920
01:06:09.854 --> 01:06:11.102
The sewage thing.

921
01:06:11.102 --> 01:06:11.934
<v ARSENEAULT>The pipe?

922
01:06:11.934 --> 01:06:14.015
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah they pipe the whole . . .

923
01:06:14.015 --> 01:06:19.423
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>They put a sewage down in Bel Air and Bel Air didn't have

924
01:06:19.423 --> 01:06:19.839
<c.p>sewage.

925
01:06:19.839 --> 01:06:21.504
Mossville didn't have sewage.

926
01:06:21.504 --> 01:06:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When did y'all get sewage?

927
01:06:24.000 --> 01:06:27.386
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Bel Air got it first, and then the next . . . Now,

928
01:06:27.386 --> 01:06:33.480
after they finish all of the sewage in Bel Air, then they found out there was a spill.

929
01:06:33.480 --> 01:06:34.157
<v DELMA BENNETT>The spill.

930
01:06:34.157 --> 01:06:37.882
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>And they moved everybody, but they knew they were coming though.

931
01:06:37.882 --> 01:06:42.283
They knew they had to have sewage to make their business go, too.

932
01:06:42.283 --> 01:06:44.654
So they fixed it up for themselves.

933
01:06:44.654 --> 01:06:46.346
<v DELMA BENNETT>Did you see Toxic Town?

934
01:06:46.346 --> 01:06:48.378
You see on Toxic Town they . . .

935
01:06:48.378 --> 01:06:48.717
<v ARSENEAULT>Documentary?

936
01:06:48.717 --> 01:06:49.055
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>[Agrees]

937
01:06:49.055 --> 01:06:49.394
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah.

938
01:06:49.394 --> 01:06:53.795
It tells that the . . . It was even in the waters at the plants.

939
01:06:53.795 --> 01:06:58.197
This is how much they're concerned about the people that they work for.

940
01:06:58.197 --> 01:07:04.969
There’s people that work at the plant don't know nothing about the danger that they're in working at the plant.

941
01:07:04.969 --> 01:07:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Even in the water fountain?

942
01:07:07.000 --> 01:07:09.184
<v DELMA BENNETT>It was in the water fountain.

943
01:07:09.184 --> 01:07:10.640
They were drinking it.

944
01:07:10.640 --> 01:07:15.008
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Until they realize it and then they got them all bottled water.

945
01:07:15.008 --> 01:07:21.195
But they don't drink it like right now since they know the water was in the plant.

946
01:07:21.195 --> 01:07:26.655
They get bottled water, but some of the men are still taking showers out there.

947
01:07:26.655 --> 01:07:27.019
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

948
01:07:27.019 --> 01:07:32.479
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>But you know a man get hurt at the plant they get a toxicologist immediately?

949
01:07:32.479 --> 01:07:37.575
They have them with a toxicologist, but they will not let us get one.

950
01:07:37.575 --> 01:07:38.303
Every time.

951
01:07:38.303 --> 01:07:41.215
I went all the way to Chicago, Illinois.

952
01:07:41.215 --> 01:07:43.398
His name was Doctor Peter [Ores?].

953
01:07:43.398 --> 01:07:52.862
He came here and tested the ten or thirty-something people and they gave the results of what happened in . . . To those people that high dioxin level.

954
01:07:52.862 --> 01:08:00.870
That was . . . Over thirty-something people had the highest in the United States, in the country they said when that toxicologist tested them.

955
01:08:00.870 --> 01:08:03.418
Well I went to that same toxicologist.

956
01:08:03.418 --> 01:08:10.697
I went all the way to Chicago and he was like . . . It was on a Monday I went to him.

957
01:08:10.697 --> 01:08:15.793
That Tuesday he set up to test me to find out my blood count.

958
01:08:15.793 --> 01:08:21.253
When I went to his office that Tuesday morning he say, "Are you from Mossville?"

959
01:08:21.253 --> 01:08:23.801
I said, "What difference does it make?

960
01:08:23.801 --> 01:08:25.257
I'm here in Chicago."

961
01:08:25.257 --> 01:08:27.805
He says, "But are you from Mossville?"

962
01:08:27.805 --> 01:08:31.444
I said, "Well I ain't got to lie to you.

963
01:08:31.444 --> 01:08:32.536
Yes, I am.

964
01:08:32.536 --> 01:08:35.812
I'm from Mossville, but I'm here in Chicago now."

965
01:08:35.812 --> 01:08:38.360
He says, "Well let's reschedule this appointment."

966
01:08:38.360 --> 01:08:40.908
He didn't do the tests on me."

967
01:08:40.908 --> 01:08:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Why not?

968
01:08:42.000 --> 01:08:43.071
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Never said why.

969
01:08:43.071 --> 01:08:45.929
When he found out I was from Mossville.

970
01:08:45.929 --> 01:08:49.500
It was like he was told not to do it.

971
01:08:49.500 --> 01:08:54.857
He was taken back to Chicago and never stepped back foot in this area again.

972
01:08:54.857 --> 01:08:58.071
Dr. Peter [Ores?] never came back to Mossville again.

973
01:08:58.071 --> 01:09:02.357
It was like industry buy . . . And we just . . . I'm just speculating again.

974
01:09:02.357 --> 01:09:09.857
It's just like every time you get somebody . . . It's just like every time we get ready to have an industry meeting,

975
01:09:09.857 --> 01:09:18.071
we were getting ready to have one at Memorial Hospital when they found out it was for industry . . . It's like partners in education.

976
01:09:18.071 --> 01:09:22.357
You can't go nowhere the school board will let you do anything.

977
01:09:22.357 --> 01:09:24.500
Just like our police juror said.

978
01:09:24.500 --> 01:09:31.286
Now, we put him in office for the people, but he said if we say anything, Mr. [Hal] McMillin,

979
01:09:31.286 --> 01:09:31.486
if we got anything to say against industries, he don't want to have nothing to . . .

980
01:09:31.286 --> 01:09:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>[. . .?]

981
01:09:37.000 --> 01:09:37.395
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Yeah.

982
01:09:37.395 --> 01:09:41.344
He say he wouldn't have nothing to do with that.

983
01:09:41.344 --> 01:09:51.612
Then on top of that, just the other day I called KPLC so I asked them could we do a talk because everything you was hearing,

984
01:09:51.612 --> 01:09:55.167
how wonderful Sasol is bringing economy to the community,

985
01:09:55.167 --> 01:10:02.275
but you never hear what it done to Mossville and the health that people are fighting from industries.

986
01:10:02.275 --> 01:10:08.594
The man told me he could not let me get on T.V. and talk like that.

987
01:10:08.594 --> 01:10:15.703
I said, "But you you’re your program was to . . . anybody can call and make statements to better things.”

988
01:10:15.703 --> 01:10:28.736
And I just wanted to share with the people the stuff that Wilma Subra has given us as a community of Mossville and how industries are buying out us and what they're doing.

989
01:10:28.736 --> 01:10:32.290
He said he couldn't let me get on T.V.

990
01:10:32.290 --> 01:10:33.475
<c.p>and do that.

991
01:10:33.475 --> 01:10:36.239
Why everybody afraid to go against industry?

992
01:10:36.239 --> 01:10:37.819
Why everybody don't want . . .?

993
01:10:37.819 --> 01:10:43.743
You can say anything good about industries, but you can't say nothing bad about them.

994
01:10:43.743 --> 01:10:48.877
It's sad that people don't realize that it ain't just Mossville that's dying.

995
01:10:48.877 --> 01:10:55.986
We have people that tell us, "Well my family got cancer and I don't blame it on industry."

996
01:10:55.986 --> 01:10:57.565
Well you know what?

997
01:10:57.565 --> 01:10:59.935
Ain't . . . every cancer is from industry,

998
01:10:59.935 --> 01:11:07.833
but it's going to play a role because when Wilma Subra and a lot of scientists and chemistries, and biologies,

999
01:11:07.833 --> 01:11:16.522
and everything that's been going on found out every step of what each one of these plants are putting out from headaches,

1000
01:11:16.522 --> 01:11:22.051
from asthma, they got a . . . Every last one of them is broke down now.

1001
01:11:22.051 --> 01:11:32.024
So why you going to sit here and say . . . We have people that move from Saint Luscious in the last three years.

1002
01:11:32.024 --> 01:11:40.366
The woman barely can breathe since she's here because our air is so . . . Right here in Lake Charles.

1003
01:11:40.366 --> 01:11:43.610
Because the air blows over here, too.

1004
01:11:43.610 --> 01:11:50.561
With that whole area going to be industries Lake Charles going to be affected too,

1005
01:11:50.561 --> 01:11:56.585
but they don't want you to get on T.V. and tell people that.

1006
01:11:56.585 --> 01:12:01.220
Even causing birth defects and kid’s heart . . . Having learning disabilities.

1007
01:12:01.220 --> 01:12:03.073
A lot of these . . .

1008
01:12:03.073 --> 01:12:04.000
<v DELMA BENNETT>Chemicals.

1009
01:12:04.000 --> 01:12:13.321
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Chemicals that are coming out at the plant is a cause of it, but nobody won't let us get on T.V. and warn it.

1010
01:12:13.321 --> 01:12:16.040
That's just like about the Dollar Stores.

1011
01:12:16.040 --> 01:12:21.478
That the man came to Mossville and told us that Dollar Tree, Family Dollar,

1012
01:12:21.478 --> 01:12:29.246
and Dollar General all the dollar stores are . . . have . . . majority of their baby products like the pacifiers and the bottles,

1013
01:12:29.246 --> 01:12:30.799
have lead in it.

1014
01:12:30.799 --> 01:12:33.518
Why couldn't we put that out there?

1015
01:12:33.518 --> 01:12:41.286
Why couldn't we warn the people to be careful and warn our . . . There's nothing wrong with the Dollar Stores now,

1016
01:12:41.286 --> 01:12:42.839
but they always set

1017
01:12:42.839 --> 01:12:47.500
<c.p>up near African American community, or a poor Asian, or Spanish community.

1018
01:12:47.500 --> 01:12:49.830
That’s where they place them at.

1019
01:12:49.830 --> 01:12:57.210
They never put them areas where it's not . . . The rich white folk's area you never find these stores there.

1020
01:12:57.210 --> 01:12:59.929
It's always near a poor minority community.

1021
01:12:59.929 --> 01:13:03.812
The man came to Mossville and gave us that presentation.

1022
01:13:03.812 --> 01:13:10.804
They talked and they said a little bitty [certain?], but don't do nothing to mess it up now.

1023
01:13:10.804 --> 01:13:16.241
What is wrong with our news people that are . . . Everybody's worried about that dollar.

1024
01:13:16.241 --> 01:13:20.513
Nobody's worrying about people's health and our babies that's coming up.

1025
01:13:20.513 --> 01:13:23.232
Why don't somebody take interest in that?

1026
01:13:23.232 --> 01:13:24.397
But nobody cares.

1027
01:13:24.397 --> 01:13:25.562
It’s that dollar.

1028
01:13:25.562 --> 01:13:31.000
And we been trying to warn them in all kinds of ways now.

1029
01:13:31.000 --> 01:13:36.302
<v ARSENEAULT>Speaking of the dollar, I want to talk about those first buyouts.

1030
01:13:36.302 --> 01:13:38.953
What do y'all remember about that?

1031
01:13:38.953 --> 01:13:40.279
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>In Bel Air?

1032
01:13:40.279 --> 01:13:41.163
Oh, God.

1033
01:13:41.163 --> 01:13:44.256
<v DELMA BENNETT>The first buyout was a big sham.

1034
01:13:44.256 --> 01:13:44.698
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Lawyer.

1035
01:13:44.698 --> 01:13:48.233
<v DELMA BENNETT>We had never experienced anything like that before.

1036
01:13:48.233 --> 01:13:50.884
It was a class action suit.

1037
01:13:50.884 --> 01:14:09.000
And the class action suit is where a lawyer came in and he had some black representatives from his firm to come in and talk to the different people in the community about selling their property in the class action suit.

1038
01:14:09.000 --> 01:14:18.279
We had meetings and stuff where judges and lawyers came in to advise us on the selling of the class action.

1039
01:14:18.279 --> 01:14:18.479
What happened was that the . . .

1040
01:14:18.279 --> 01:14:20.488
<v ARSENEAULT>[ . . .?]

1041
01:14:20.488 --> 01:14:21.814
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Oh the telephone.

1042
01:14:21.814 --> 01:14:23.581
What time is it?

1043
01:14:23.581 --> 01:14:24.465
<v DELMA BENNETT>They actually . . .

1044
01:14:24.465 --> 01:14:26.233
<v ARSENEAULT>You got to go?

1045
01:14:26.233 --> 01:14:28.000
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>[disagrees] Three o'clock.

1046
01:14:28.000 --> 01:14:29.613
<v DELMA BENNETT>They actually shammed us.

1047
01:14:29.613 --> 01:14:37.679
They really shammed us because they gave us what they think we deserved because it was two communities involved, now.

1048
01:14:37.679 --> 01:14:40.099
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>I want to catch that phone.

1049
01:14:40.099 --> 01:14:42.115
<v DELMA BENNETT>There was two communities involved.

1050
01:14:42.115 --> 01:14:46.551
There was the white community, and there was the black community.

1051
01:14:46.551 --> 01:14:55.021
Now I don't know if the white community was in the class action or not, but they left with no problem.

1052
01:14:55.021 --> 01:14:56.634
You ain't heard nothing.

1053
01:14:56.634 --> 01:15:01.877
You ain't had one word about the white community and what they got.

1054
01:15:01.877 --> 01:15:14.782
I know that one guy in the white community that was right behind Conoco that was in the travel of the plume that was going south because everything heads toward the gulf,

1055
01:15:14.782 --> 01:15:21.638
and when he sold his property he got a million dollars off the top for his property.

1056
01:15:21.638 --> 01:15:24.058
They moved all of his shrubs.

1057
01:15:24.058 --> 01:15:26.477
They moved his shrubs on and

1058
01:15:26.477 --> 01:15:27.284
<c.p>eighteen wheelers.

1059
01:15:27.284 --> 01:15:31.720
All his crepe myrtles and all the trees that they wanted.

1060
01:15:31.720 --> 01:15:36.963
They dug them up and moved it to the place where he was.

1061
01:15:36.963 --> 01:15:44.222
Now the people in the Bel Air area, the most that anybody in the black community was offered,

1062
01:15:44.222 --> 01:15:48.255
I think it was a quarter of a million dollars.

1063
01:15:48.255 --> 01:15:49.868
In the black community.

1064
01:15:49.868 --> 01:15:54.708
They had a man that live right across the street from Vista.

1065
01:15:54.708 --> 01:15:59.547
They would to give him but a quarter of a million dollars.

1066
01:15:59.547 --> 01:16:04.387
He had exotic birds and all kind of stuff in his community.

1067
01:16:04.387 --> 01:16:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was that?

1068
01:16:06.000 --> 01:16:09.368
<v DELMA BENNETT>His name was Prince His name was Prince.

1069
01:16:09.368 --> 01:16:11.053
<v ARSENEAULT>What's his first name?

1070
01:16:11.053 --> 01:16:11.474
David?

1071
01:16:11.474 --> 01:16:11.895
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>David.

1072
01:16:11.895 --> 01:16:12.737
<v DELMA BENNETT>David Prince.

1073
01:16:12.737 --> 01:16:14.842
His wife died of cancer.

1074
01:16:14.842 --> 01:16:22.000
Now, he died from a fire that took place when he moved from that area into Mossville.

1075
01:16:22.000 --> 01:16:24.947
But that's the type of . . . and Lundy.

1076
01:16:24.947 --> 01:16:30.842
Lundy and his associates was the ones that was responsible for the class action.

1077
01:16:30.842 --> 01:16:34.211
They made millions of dollars off of it.

1078
01:16:34.211 --> 01:16:35.474
<v ARSENEAULT>The lawyers did?

1079
01:16:35.474 --> 01:16:39.263
<v DELMA BENNETT>The lawyers made millions of dollars off of it.

1080
01:16:39.263 --> 01:16:48.105
Even the ones that they had to come and . . . they send into . . . The Ringo and his wife and all them. [Madame

1081
01:16:48.105 --> 01:16:54.000
<c.p>Gospel?], they made . . . They call them Madame Gospel I don't know the real name.

1082
01:16:54.000 --> 01:16:57.368
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Y'all not going to put all that now. [. . .?]

1083
01:16:57.368 --> 01:17:01.158
<v DELMA BENNETT>The word Madame Gospel, she was on the radio.

1084
01:17:01.158 --> 01:17:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1085
01:17:02.000 --> 01:17:04.620
<v DELMA BENNETT>You know she was on the radio . . .

1086
01:17:04.620 --> 01:17:06.491
<v ARSENEAULT>She was a radio host?

1087
01:17:06.491 --> 01:17:06.866
<v DELMA BENNETT>Right.

1088
01:17:06.866 --> 01:17:10.609
But she worked with them and they ended up rich.

1089
01:17:10.609 --> 01:17:11.731
They left rich.

1090
01:17:11.731 --> 01:17:13.603
Lundy and them left rich.

1091
01:17:13.603 --> 01:17:15.474
They put some money in . . .

1092
01:17:15.474 --> 01:17:16.597
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>You know that?

1093
01:17:16.597 --> 01:17:16.971
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah.

1094
01:17:16.971 --> 01:17:18.094
I know that.

1095
01:17:18.094 --> 01:17:19.591
Do I know that?

1096
01:17:19.591 --> 01:17:20.714
They still rich.

1097
01:17:20.714 --> 01:17:22.960
They put money in McNeese [University].

1098
01:17:22.960 --> 01:17:28.949
Where the young people in Mossville who wanted to get into the industry, the chemical industry,

1099
01:17:28.949 --> 01:17:37.557
if they were going to school for a certain thing they would be able to tap into the money they put in McNeese.

1100
01:17:37.557 --> 01:17:40.926
But none . . . Nobody in Mossville ever tapped into it.

1101
01:17:40.926 --> 01:17:45.417
The ones that tried to tap into it couldn't tap into it.

1102
01:17:45.417 --> 01:17:50.657
All of it went to the other communities around us other than Mossville themselves.

1103
01:17:50.657 --> 01:17:53.651
This the kind of stuff that took place.

1104
01:17:53.651 --> 01:17:59.640
<c.p>Now, it didn't really excite me that much because the little property that I had there,

1105
01:17:59.640 --> 01:18:09.371
I thought maybe I had gotten a good deal for my property because of the fact that I hadn't really paid a lot for my property.

1106
01:18:09.371 --> 01:18:16.109
That's what I learned from what happened in Bel Air compared to what's happening in Mossville to me.

1107
01:18:16.109 --> 01:18:21.723
My property that I had was almost in the heart of what they're doing there.

1108
01:18:21.723 --> 01:18:30.706
They knew ahead of time when they bought Bel Air that they was coming into Bel Air the way that they coming in now.

1109
01:18:30.706 --> 01:18:37.817
They knew that because this thing was in the works many years before it even got into the works.

1110
01:18:37.817 --> 01:18:41.186
They had done planned all this in South Africa.

1111
01:18:41.186 --> 01:18:46.426
How they was going to come in to Mossville and do what they done.

1112
01:18:46.426 --> 01:18:55.409
Because a girl by the name of Shirley has showed me the map of the industrial move that they was making in this area.

1113
01:18:55.409 --> 01:18:59.526
It's supposed to extend all the way to Post Oak Road.

1114
01:18:59.526 --> 01:19:03.643
That's how far they supposed to go with this industrial movement.

1115
01:19:03.643 --> 01:19:06.637
They haven't gotten into the other section yet.

1116
01:19:06.637 --> 01:19:13.000
I guess they're waiting for Sasol to start off so they can continue to start moving.

1117
01:19:13.000 --> 01:19:20.200
So that's why they putting us on a volunteer base now because when they get started they going really explode.

1118
01:19:20.200 --> 01:19:30.280
And when they explode . . . They even building . . . Overpasses and all this kind of stuff in Mossville to get back and forth to their plants and stuff like that.

1119
01:19:30.280 --> 01:19:34.240
So we're not needed now, but we eventually we’ll be needed,

1120
01:19:34.240 --> 01:19:40.720
and if we can hold out long enough we might can get what we want, but I can.

1121
01:19:40.720 --> 01:19:49.360
I can because God has blessed me to be able to come here and trying to buy another place without their help at all.

1122
01:19:49.360 --> 01:19:52.600
But it's just the idea that we're played on.

1123
01:19:52.600 --> 01:19:56.560
We're played on and we don't have anybody to represent us.

1124
01:19:56.560 --> 01:19:58.720
And the lawyers that we get,

1125
01:19:58.720 --> 01:20:06.280
they can only do what we tell them to do because we can't get lawyers that we can afford to pay,

1126
01:20:06.280 --> 01:20:13.120
and we can't get lawyers that we can afford to pay because nobody not going to take the cases.

1127
01:20:13.120 --> 01:20:16.000
They would not fight against the plants.

1128
01:20:16.000 --> 01:20:19.000
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Now Debra Sullivan is the one can tell you about that.

1129
01:20:19.000 --> 01:20:20.364
<v DELMA BENNETT>She can break it down.

1130
01:20:20.364 --> 01:20:22.273
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>She was the leader of that one.

1131
01:20:22.273 --> 01:20:23.364
<v ARSENEAULT>Of the first buyout?

1132
01:20:23.364 --> 01:20:24.182
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>The first buyout.

1133
01:20:24.182 --> 01:20:25.545
She was definitely the leader.

1134
01:20:25.545 --> 01:20:27.727
<v DELMA BENNETT>She got papers and all kinds of stuff.

1135
01:20:27.727 --> 01:20:30.182
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>She got all kind of stuff what they did.

1136
01:20:30.182 --> 01:20:34.000
And it was a sad situation what happened, but we learned from that.

1137
01:20:34.000 --> 01:20:34.309
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yes.

1138
01:20:34.309 --> 01:20:36.164
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Because we don't have no lawyers.

1139
01:20:36.164 --> 01:20:36.473
We . . .

1140
01:20:36.473 --> 01:20:37.400
<v DELMA BENNETT>Can't get none.

1141
01:20:37.400 --> 01:20:44.818
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Oh they got some want to take us, but we refuse to have any because of what they done to people in Bel Air.

1142
01:20:44.818 --> 01:20:45.436
Refuse it.

1143
01:20:45.436 --> 01:20:47.600
And we don't know how it’s going . . .

1144
01:20:47.600 --> 01:20:50.382
<c.p>We don't know . . . why we going to need one.

1145
01:20:50.382 --> 01:20:54.400
We don't know what's going to happen because now if you notice K.C.

1146
01:20:54.400 --> 01:20:56.255
Railroad, they moving up now, too.

1147
01:20:56.255 --> 01:20:58.418
They getting ready to add six tracks.

1148
01:20:58.418 --> 01:21:02.436
Why all of a sudden they just decided they need six tracks now?

1149
01:21:02.436 --> 01:21:04.600
Because industries and railroad is working together.

1150
01:21:04.600 --> 01:21:06.455
They going to need each other.

1151
01:21:06.455 --> 01:21:09.545
Not only is that . . . You hear all kind of talk.

1152
01:21:09.545 --> 01:21:14.491
I don't know if you done heard about bridge need fixing, but it can't get fixed.

1153
01:21:14.491 --> 01:21:18.200
The I-10 can't get fixed because of all the . . . What is it?

1154
01:21:18.200 --> 01:21:19.127
<v DELMA BENNETT>[Sediment?] in it.

1155
01:21:19.127 --> 01:21:19.745
<v ARSENEAULT>The corrosion?

1156
01:21:19.745 --> 01:21:20.055
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Yeah.

1157
01:21:20.055 --> 01:21:21.291
They can't disturb it.

1158
01:21:21.291 --> 01:21:22.218
<v DELMA BENNETT>Not the corrosion.

1159
01:21:22.218 --> 01:21:23.764
The sediment from the plant.

1160
01:21:23.764 --> 01:21:25.000
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>No the sediment.

1161
01:21:25.000 --> 01:21:29.809
<v DELMA BENNETT>The sediment from the plants that have been put into the river for years.

1162
01:21:29.809 --> 01:21:30.496
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>The E.D.C.

1163
01:21:30.496 --> 01:21:31.183
<v DELMA BENNETT>And years.

1164
01:21:31.183 --> 01:21:32.214
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>E.D.C. is underneath.

1165
01:21:32.214 --> 01:21:40.458
<v DELMA BENNETT>They have sunk down into the bottom, and if you start disturbing it it's going to work it all back up into the water.

1166
01:21:40.458 --> 01:21:46.298
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>But then if you notice in Westlake, Sasol is coming all the way up to the water.

1167
01:21:46.298 --> 01:21:52.137
Now they got big barges coming in because this is a company coming dealing with South Africa.

1168
01:21:52.137 --> 01:21:57.977
So they got to have a waterway to get from there to what they got to do.

1169
01:21:57.977 --> 01:22:03.130
They going to drudge up a lot of stuff, but ain't nobody thinking about that.

1170
01:22:03.130 --> 01:22:04.160
We in trouble.

1171
01:22:04.160 --> 01:22:10.000
We in trouble with our water system right here in Lake Charles what's fixing to happen.

1172
01:22:10.000 --> 01:22:16.049
<v DELMA BENNETT>And they tell you right now the fish that you eat from that water, you eat it at your own risk.

1173
01:22:16.049 --> 01:22:17.489
<v ARSENEAULT>They tell you that now?

1174
01:22:17.489 --> 01:22:24.114
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah they got the areas where they say if you eat the fish from that area, you eat it at your own risk.

1175
01:22:24.114 --> 01:22:27.859
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Not only that, they out here playing in that water on the beach.

1176
01:22:27.859 --> 01:22:30.451
When you ever seen some gray and black water?

1177
01:22:30.451 --> 01:22:35.924
Water was blue at time and greenish, but now it's horrible and people out there playing in that water.

1178
01:22:35.924 --> 01:22:40.245
If they would have messed with the bridge that beach would have been in trouble.

1179
01:22:40.245 --> 01:22:41.109
<v DELMA BENNETT>Not only that.

1180
01:22:41.109 --> 01:22:48.886
They say that if you go in that water with a cut or something you can get a flesh-eating disease from being in that water and stuff.

1181
01:22:48.886 --> 01:22:49.462
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Right now.

1182
01:22:49.462 --> 01:22:51.478
<v DELMA BENNETT>They was advertising that on television itself.

1183
01:22:51.478 --> 01:22:52.630
<v ARSENEAULT>I've actually heard that.

1184
01:22:52.630 --> 01:22:52.918
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Yeah.

1185
01:22:52.918 --> 01:22:53.207
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah.

1186
01:22:53.207 --> 01:22:58.967
<v ARSENEAULT>I wanted to ask y'all some more about Mr. Valery and The Paradise Club while I got you both here.

1187
01:22:58.967 --> 01:23:00.696
What do y'all remember about that?

1188
01:23:00.696 --> 01:23:03.000
<v DELMA BENNETT>Well she don't know much about that.

1189
01:23:03.000 --> 01:23:05.103
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>My mother and father went [laughs].

1190
01:23:05.103 --> 01:23:08.957
And like he said, my . . . He brought in James Brown, B.B.

1191
01:23:08.957 --> 01:23:13.863
King, Bobby . . . he brought in some people and that's our only entertainment we had.

1192
01:23:13.863 --> 01:23:16.667
They had like another place called Joy Hill.

1193
01:23:16.667 --> 01:23:18.419
<v DELMA BENNETT>They had a [motel?] too.

1194
01:23:18.419 --> 01:23:18.769
Yeah.

1195
01:23:18.769 --> 01:23:26.479
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>We had Joy Hill, but Joy Hill was just a little night club, that not too many people went to Joy Hill.

1196
01:23:26.479 --> 01:23:32.085
But Paradise was the only club and there was another little . . . Ms. Patty Moss had one.

1197
01:23:32.085 --> 01:23:32.436
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah.

1198
01:23:32.436 --> 01:23:35.239
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>We had one down on Patty Moss Road.

1199
01:23:35.239 --> 01:23:40.145
Little places where people would go and drink at, but Paradise was the place.

1200
01:23:40.145 --> 01:23:40.846
And Miss . . .

1201
01:23:40.846 --> 01:23:42.598
<v ARSENEAULT>What did it look like?

1202
01:23:42.598 --> 01:23:44.000
Do you remember?

1203
01:23:44.000 --> 01:23:45.528
<v DELMA BENNETT>It was a two-story building.

1204
01:23:45.528 --> 01:23:45.833
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Yeah.

1205
01:23:45.833 --> 01:23:46.444
Two-story building.

1206
01:23:46.444 --> 01:23:48.278
<v DELMA BENNETT>With a . . . They had a balcony.

1207
01:23:48.278 --> 01:23:49.500
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>He had a balcony.

1208
01:23:49.500 --> 01:23:49.806
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah.

1209
01:23:49.806 --> 01:23:51.028
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>They even had rooms.

1210
01:23:51.028 --> 01:23:54.694
You could rent . . . It was like a little hotel in there, too.

1211
01:23:54.694 --> 01:23:55.611
It had everything.

1212
01:23:55.611 --> 01:23:57.750
It was a restaurant, part of it.

1213
01:23:57.750 --> 01:23:58.667
It was nice.

1214
01:23:58.667 --> 01:23:59.889
It was real nice.

1215
01:23:59.889 --> 01:24:00.500
It was

1216
01:24:00.500 --> 01:24:02.333
<c.p>. . . And it had teen dances there.

1217
01:24:02.333 --> 01:24:03.861
They had wedding parties there.

1218
01:24:03.861 --> 01:24:05.694
Mr. Valery had something going on.

1219
01:24:05.694 --> 01:24:06.917
He had something nice.

1220
01:24:06.917 --> 01:24:08.750
<v DELMA BENNETT>He had a nice little thing.

1221
01:24:08.750 --> 01:24:11.500
He lived next door to it at the time.

1222
01:24:11.500 --> 01:24:13.944
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Yeah he used to live right next door.

1223
01:24:13.944 --> 01:24:14.861
<v DELMA BENNETT>It was really . . .

1224
01:24:14.861 --> 01:24:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And his wife was a cook?

1225
01:24:17.000 --> 01:24:17.761
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>The best.

1226
01:24:17.761 --> 01:24:18.903
The best cook.

1227
01:24:18.903 --> 01:24:24.611
He did it every . . . She took care a lot of homeless people, Mr. Valery's wife.

1228
01:24:24.611 --> 01:24:29.177
And when they left from there they built a hundred bedroom house.

1229
01:24:29.177 --> 01:24:29.558
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah.

1230
01:24:29.558 --> 01:24:31.080
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Where they at now.

1231
01:24:31.080 --> 01:24:33.363
They always took in homeless people.

1232
01:24:33.363 --> 01:24:33.743
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah.

1233
01:24:33.743 --> 01:24:35.265
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Or always helping somebody.

1234
01:24:35.265 --> 01:24:36.788
<v DELMA BENNETT>He's a nice guy.

1235
01:24:36.788 --> 01:24:38.310
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>They always helping somebody.

1236
01:24:38.310 --> 01:24:57.717
So The Paradise was really a very . . . because . . . and just amazing that you ask because they were just telling us that was a write-up back in . . . way back when my . . . They had a fight at The Paradise and they was Lake Charles against Mossville and my brothers were in that fight.

1237
01:24:57.717 --> 01:25:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it over a girl?

1238
01:25:00.000 --> 01:25:06.889
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Over a girl. [laughs] Over a girl, but they had quite a . . . It was quite a lot of entertainment there.

1239
01:25:06.889 --> 01:25:12.744
Because the night that they had the fight I wasn't supposed to be with him that night.

1240
01:25:12.744 --> 01:25:18.944
I was so glad that fight happened because my daddy didn't have a chance to fuss at me.

1241
01:25:18.944 --> 01:25:20.322
<v ARSENEAULT>Fuss at you, yeah.

1242
01:25:20.322 --> 01:25:21.700
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>So it was cute.

1243
01:25:21.700 --> 01:25:24.111
It was a lot of going on.

1244
01:25:24.111 --> 01:25:26.522
It was a big dance that night.

1245
01:25:26.522 --> 01:25:27.556
It was excitement.

1246
01:25:27.556 --> 01:25:31.000
So when things happen in Mossville there wasn't no secret.

1247
01:25:31.000 --> 01:25:32.378
Everybody knew about it.

1248
01:25:32.378 --> 01:25:35.822
So the Paradise was just . . . That was our entertainment place.

1249
01:25:35.822 --> 01:25:38.578
That's all we had for real big entertainment.

1250
01:25:38.578 --> 01:25:39.956
<v ARSENEAULT>What about growing up?

1251
01:25:39.956 --> 01:25:44.433
What kind of things would y'all do for fun when you were young?

1252
01:25:44.433 --> 01:25:48.222
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>When we . . . we had a lot of . . . My father was a . . .

1253
01:25:48.222 --> 01:25:48.567
<v DELMA BENNETT>Tyrant.

1254
01:25:48.567 --> 01:25:49.944
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>[laughs] He was strict.

1255
01:25:49.944 --> 01:25:51.322
<v ARSENEAULT>He was a tyrant?

1256
01:25:51.322 --> 01:25:52.356
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>He was strict.

1257
01:25:52.356 --> 01:25:55.456
We didn't go nowhere and we were six sisters.

1258
01:25:55.456 --> 01:25:57.178
Well none of us really.

1259
01:25:57.178 --> 01:26:00.278
He didn't even allow my brothers to go nowhere.

1260
01:26:00.278 --> 01:26:04.411
He just didn't believe in that night life, but he did it.

1261
01:26:04.411 --> 01:26:07.511
They went to Valery's, but they wouldn't let us.

1262
01:26:07.511 --> 01:26:08.889
They had record hops.

1263
01:26:08.889 --> 01:26:14.056
We had sock hops, record hops, weenie roasts, and somebody always was throwing a party.

1264
01:26:14.056 --> 01:26:20.600
Because the recreation was always there to have a record hop, swimming parties, we did a lot of sports.

1265
01:26:20.600 --> 01:26:22.667
A lot of sports in Mossville.

1266
01:26:22.667 --> 01:26:24.389
That was football, baseball, basketball.

1267
01:26:24.389 --> 01:26:27.144
It was a lot went on in Mossville.

1268
01:26:27.144 --> 01:26:29.211
It was always something to do.

1269
01:26:29.211 --> 01:26:33.000
We played a lot of games like hiding people and . . .

1270
01:26:33.000 --> 01:26:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a best friend?

1271
01:26:35.000 --> 01:26:37.333
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Yeah that's what I was telling you.

1272
01:26:37.333 --> 01:26:42.000
It was just amazing she was Peggy Frank and she's coming home next week.

1273
01:26:42.000 --> 01:26:48.333
She lived next door to us and it was three of us . . . It was four of us all together.

1274
01:26:48.333 --> 01:26:50.333
Was me, Margie, Peggy, and Katy.

1275
01:26:50.333 --> 01:26:52.000
We all grew up together.

1276
01:26:52.000 --> 01:26:56.333
Went to Mossville together and when they came over integration it separated us.

1277
01:26:56.333 --> 01:27:01.667
Three of us went to Westlake and Margie went . . . No, all of us went to Westlake.

1278
01:27:01.667 --> 01:27:03.667
All of us wound up going

1279
01:27:03.667 --> 01:27:04.667
<c.p>to Westlake High.

1280
01:27:04.667 --> 01:27:08.333
The rest of our friends had to go to Sulphur High.

1281
01:27:08.333 --> 01:27:13.000
But other than that yeah we all was like . . . We grew up as sisters.

1282
01:27:13.000 --> 01:27:16.333
We were right in each other house everyday just about.

1283
01:27:16.333 --> 01:27:19.333
We'd come home from school, get out our clothes,

1284
01:27:19.333 --> 01:27:33.333
we had a . . . always somebody had a . . . My dad had a big field on the side of the house and we would set up a baseball game or I was a track winner so I ran all the boys in the community.

1285
01:27:33.333 --> 01:27:43.333
I was the tomboy and I used to get out . . . We'd get out in the middle of the road and we run each other to see who was the fastest.

1286
01:27:43.333 --> 01:27:46.000
So Mossville always had some clean fun.

1287
01:27:46.000 --> 01:27:50.861
But the main thing that the losing of Mossville was how it happened.

1288
01:27:50.861 --> 01:27:57.217
They just like, came in one night and the next day they was buying out the people.

1289
01:27:57.217 --> 01:28:00.209
Didn't even give us a chance to talk.

1290
01:28:00.209 --> 01:28:09.183
Didn't even give us a chance to call a community meeting before they start buying out people, that's what's the painful thing about it.

1291
01:28:09.183 --> 01:28:16.661
Why didn't they just call a big community meeting and let us know that this is what's fixing to happen?

1292
01:28:16.661 --> 01:28:22.643
They started out undercover buying out land, offering people money, and didn't even tell us nothing.

1293
01:28:22.643 --> 01:28:24.887
So I said that was painful.

1294
01:28:24.887 --> 01:28:26.383
That was really painful.

1295
01:28:26.383 --> 01:28:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did y'all go to church?

1296
01:28:29.000 --> 01:28:44.000
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Most of the . . . they got . . .the churches in Mossville . . . Mossville was covered in churches and we didn't go because we were Catholic so our church was . . . My church was at Saint John Bosco.

1297
01:28:44.000 --> 01:28:51.273
So we went to Saint John Bosco and we even had problems with racial problems there.

1298
01:28:51.273 --> 01:28:54.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it a segregated church?

1299
01:28:54.000 --> 01:29:01.326
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>The whites sat on one side and the black sits on . . . The whites sit in the front, the blacks sits on the side.

1300
01:29:01.326 --> 01:29:04.511
But we came up and we broke that up, too.

1301
01:29:04.511 --> 01:29:07.378
I got slapped by the priest on the bus.

1302
01:29:07.378 --> 01:29:10.244
And I'm like, "Wow I must have been terrible.

1303
01:29:10.244 --> 01:29:12.156
I got slapped by the priest."

1304
01:29:12.156 --> 01:29:16.933
Because the kids . . . When we get to catechism we would always go to the store.

1305
01:29:16.933 --> 01:29:24.578
And when we came back and I was getting on the bus the priest came and stood up on the bus and he said,

1306
01:29:24.578 --> 01:29:28.081
"Some of you guys went to the store and y'all stole."

1307
01:29:28.081 --> 01:29:33.815
And he said . . . No he said, "You guys went to the store and there was some stealing done.

1308
01:29:33.815 --> 01:29:36.363
So whoever you are you need to confess."

1309
01:29:36.363 --> 01:29:38.911
I said, "Are you saying that we stole?

1310
01:29:38.911 --> 01:29:42.415
You're accusing us or are you accusing somebody on the bus?"

1311
01:29:42.415 --> 01:29:45.281
He said, "No, they said y'all did some stealing."

1312
01:29:45.281 --> 01:29:46.237
I stood up.

1313
01:29:46.237 --> 01:29:53.244
I say, "No, I don't appreciate you standing up on the bus saying that I stole because I don't have to steal,”

1314
01:29:53.244 --> 01:29:55.474
again like it was at the store.

1315
01:29:55.474 --> 01:29:56.748
And he slapped me.

1316
01:29:56.748 --> 01:29:59.296
He said, "You don't talk back to me."

1317
01:29:59.296 --> 01:30:05.348
I laughed because five minutes later I called my radical brother who was with me in the picket before.

1318
01:30:05.348 --> 01:30:06.941
And we had him gone.

1319
01:30:06.941 --> 01:30:09.489
We had that priest out of that church.

1320
01:30:09.489 --> 01:30:12.993
So I told them it just take standing up being right.

1321
01:30:12.993 --> 01:30:14.585
We had the priest gone.

1322
01:30:14.585 --> 01:30:15.859
He had to go.

1323
01:30:15.859 --> 01:30:17.133
His name was [Dollen?].

1324
01:30:17.133 --> 01:30:19.044
You know what they call him?

1325
01:30:19.044 --> 01:30:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Father Dollen?

1326
01:30:20.000 --> 01:30:20.337
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Yeah.

1327
01:30:20.337 --> 01:30:21.012
He's Dollen.

1328
01:30:21.012 --> 01:30:21.686
Mr. Dollen.

1329
01:30:21.686 --> 01:30:27.081
They had to move him into another area because he . . . That was wrong what he did.

1330
01:30:27.081 --> 01:30:29.779
He accused us . . . The whole bus for stealing.

1331
01:30:29.779 --> 01:30:36.523
I said, "Now I won't say somebody on here didn't go in there and steal something, but don't accuse me.

1332
01:30:36.523 --> 01:30:41.919
Y'all can sit here and let him stand up there and accuse us, but I'm not."

1333
01:30:41.919 --> 01:30:44.279
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about the protests?

1334
01:30:44.279 --> 01:30:49.000
Where there any in Mossville or was it just like in Lake Charles?

1335
01:30:49.000 --> 01:30:49.622
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>Our protests?

1336
01:30:49.622 --> 01:30:49.933
<v ARSENEAULT>Protests.

1337
01:30:49.933 --> 01:30:51.178
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>There was a protest?

1338
01:30:51.178 --> 01:30:53.044
<v ARSENEAULT>Just anything during the civil rights?

1339
01:30:53.044 --> 01:30:53.667
Protests or . . .?

1340
01:30:53.667 --> 01:30:55.844
<v CHRISTINE BENNETT>I don’t know, maybe in Lake Charles.

1341
01:30:55.844 --> 01:31:00.822
<v DELMA BENNETT>No, we just heard a lot about it with Martin Luther King was marching and stuff.

1342
01:31:00.822 --> 01:31:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about him?

1343
01:31:03.000 --> 01:31:06.211
<v DELMA BENNETT>I remember everything about Martin Luther King.

1344
01:31:06.211 --> 01:31:19.516
We had a bus that came through and some of our . . . Some of the people in Lake Charles had got on it and from Montgomery . . . the march in Montgomery.

1345
01:31:19.516 --> 01:31:35.573
I really didn't get too much into the movement because I was in San Francisco and I was involved mostly in the Black Panther and the other organizations that was in . . . doing different things. too.

1346
01:31:35.573 --> 01:31:37.867
I just followed Martin Luther

1347
01:31:37.867 --> 01:31:45.667
<c.p>King, but I hadn't really got into what he was doing until after what happened to him.

1348
01:31:45.667 --> 01:31:50.254
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember where you were when he was assassinated?

1349
01:31:50.254 --> 01:31:57.136
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah I was in San Francisco at the time and I'd seen it on television,

1350
01:31:57.136 --> 01:32:03.559
and I didn't think a lot about it at the time for some reason.

1351
01:32:03.559 --> 01:32:05.394
Because I . . . me myself,

1352
01:32:05.394 --> 01:32:26.039
I didn't have a whole lot of problem with segregation because being in California was a different thing for me than it was for a lot of the people here in Louisiana because I had been . . . Even in the fifties I was dealing with whites,

1353
01:32:26.039 --> 01:32:28.792
blacks, Chinese, Spanish, and different people.

1354
01:32:28.792 --> 01:32:37.050
So my outlook on it was a whole lot different from the people that was in the South.

1355
01:32:37.050 --> 01:33:00.448
Even though I was raised in the South I still didn't have the stigma that they had because I could still deal with . . . I had a white friend that . . . back in the fifties and we would walk down the street together and stuff and people thought something was wrong [door opens].

1356
01:33:00.448 --> 01:33:01.366
<v ARSENEAULT>Pause it?

1357
01:33:01.366 --> 01:33:03.201
<v DELMA BENNETT>[disagrees] That's my baby.

1358
01:33:03.201 --> 01:33:03.659
<v ARSENEAULT>Hello.

1359
01:33:03.659 --> 01:33:11.000
<v DELMA BENNETT>I mean we had our issues when I was in high school and stuff because . . .

1360
01:33:11.000 --> 01:33:13.725
<v ARSENEAULT>You went to high school in Lake Charles?

1361
01:33:13.725 --> 01:33:17.130
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yes, I went to high school here in Lake Charles.

1362
01:33:17.130 --> 01:33:25.986
We had our issues stuff like they had [Tommy Mack?] that we would battle a lot of times against the white kids and stuff like that.

1363
01:33:25.986 --> 01:33:35.181
Sometimes they would come down our streets with sticks sticking out the car with razors on it and stuff trying to cut us and stuff like that.

1364
01:33:35.181 --> 01:33:51.188
When they would do stuff like that we would go to the place where they were at [Tommy Mack?] and we’d have bags of dust and stuff and while they be eating we'd throw dust all over their cars and stuff while they be eating and stuff.

1365
01:33:51.188 --> 01:33:53.913
We had a lot of battles and stuff.

1366
01:33:53.913 --> 01:34:11.283
But when integration started and when they say that segregation was over I went over to [Tommy Mack?] and we went in to eat and stuff and I got arrested because I was supposed to have been the leader about going in and sitting down and all that kind of stuff.

1367
01:34:11.283 --> 01:34:16.391
They arrested me and stuff like that, but it didn't turn out to be anything.

1368
01:34:16.391 --> 01:34:20.819
But I lived through the time when you couldn't walk down the street.

1369
01:34:20.819 --> 01:34:27.290
If a white person come you had to get off the sidewalk and all that little kind of stuff.

1370
01:34:27.290 --> 01:34:36.145
When you would go uptown and that kind of . . . But it just never affected me as much as it did other people because like I said,

1371
01:34:36.145 --> 01:34:42.616
as a younger person I dealt with them in San Francisco and we didn't have that type of issue.

1372
01:34:42.616 --> 01:34:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When did you join the military?

1373
01:34:45.000 --> 01:34:46.432
<v DELMA BENNETT>I didn't join anything.

1374
01:34:46.432 --> 01:34:48.580
I was drafted back in '65.

1375
01:34:48.580 --> 01:34:50.370
They drafted me in '65.

1376
01:34:50.370 --> 01:34:52.518
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it like getting drafted?

1377
01:34:52.518 --> 01:34:56.814
<v DELMA BENNETT>I mean it was strange because I didn't know nothing about military.

1378
01:34:56.814 --> 01:35:05.047
What happened was when they drafted me I was here in Lake Charles and I had to go to Shreveport for my examination.

1379
01:35:05.047 --> 01:35:15.071
I took my examination in Shreveport and then I told them that I wasn't living in Lake Charles anymore because I done went in '64 instead of '65.

1380
01:35:15.071 --> 01:35:19.725
But I had . . . I told them I was going back to San Francisco,

1381
01:35:19.725 --> 01:35:24.737
so when I went back to San Francisco they drafted me from San Francisco.

1382
01:35:24.737 --> 01:35:27.243
So I went in June of '65.

1383
01:35:27.243 --> 01:35:40.130
When I left . . . all of us on the bus . . . when we took our examinations and everything we got on the bus and we was heading to Fort Ord California and we was having a good time.

1384
01:35:40.130 --> 01:35:45.142
We was drinking, and playing cards, and just a big old joke about it.

1385
01:35:45.142 --> 01:35:57.314
So we pulled up at Fort Ord and when we pulled out they told us all get in a line and all this kind of stuff and I thought it was a big joke.

1386
01:35:57.314 --> 01:36:03.757
Me, I looked at this guy came up to me and stuff I mean he was clean, too.

1387
01:36:03.757 --> 01:36:06.621
Drill Sergeant, I said this boy was clean.

1388
01:36:06.621 --> 01:36:08.769
This boy had that hat on.

1389
01:36:08.769 --> 01:36:18.435
This boy had that Smokey Bear hat on and he had it broken down and he had this fatigue jacket on tucked underneath his . . . Oh I say,

1390
01:36:18.435 --> 01:36:19.509
"Lord have mercy.

1391
01:36:19.509 --> 01:36:22.731
Whatever he is that's what I’m going to be."

1392
01:36:22.731 --> 01:36:27.385
I went in in my training and they started training us and stuff.

1393
01:36:27.385 --> 01:36:33.112
After that I just adapted to the life and I wound up being a drill sergeant.

1394
01:36:33.112 --> 01:36:34.902
Man, he just impressed me.

1395
01:36:34.902 --> 01:36:36.692
That man just impressed me.

1396
01:36:36.692 --> 01:36:38.482
His name was Sergeant Gentry.

1397
01:36:38.482 --> 01:36:41.346
I did my two years in the service.

1398
01:36:41.346 --> 01:36:44.568
Now, there was a little prejudice in the service.

1399
01:36:44.568 --> 01:36:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it integrated?

1400
01:36:46.000 --> 01:36:46.702
<v DELMA BENNETT>Oh yeah.

1401
01:36:46.702 --> 01:36:48.809
Yeah, but it was still prejudice.

1402
01:36:48.809 --> 01:36:51.618
They wanted the whites to outrank the blacks.

1403
01:36:51.618 --> 01:36:55.831
Like people that came into the service the same time with me,

1404
01:36:55.831 --> 01:37:01.449
they was being be made sergeants and stuff while I was still a PFC and stuff.

1405
01:37:01.449 --> 01:37:03.205
I didn't think it was

1406
01:37:03.205 --> 01:37:03.556
<c.p>right.

1407
01:37:03.556 --> 01:37:19.357
They even took a guy out of the office and there was a specialist and gave him a hard stripe so he can come out and . . . Took him out the office so he could train so he could get a hard stripe as a corporal.

1408
01:37:19.357 --> 01:37:24.624
And I've been wanting a corporal for the longest because once I got the corporal,

1409
01:37:24.624 --> 01:37:31.997
then I would have been able to become an acting sergeant and I'd have been able to get my Smoky Bear.

1410
01:37:31.997 --> 01:37:34.455
That's what I wanted that Smoky Bear.

1411
01:37:34.455 --> 01:37:37.264
It just took a lot out of me.

1412
01:37:37.264 --> 01:37:42.180
Even after I received it I didn't have the desire that I had anymore.

1413
01:37:42.180 --> 01:37:51.309
So soon as I got a chance to get out I just got out after my two years was over, but it was a good experience.

1414
01:37:51.309 --> 01:37:53.065
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever go overseas?

1415
01:37:53.065 --> 01:37:55.874
<v DELMA BENNETT>No. I stayed in Fort Ord two years.

1416
01:37:55.874 --> 01:37:59.385
Same . . . I did my basic training at one unit, C2-1.

1417
01:37:59.385 --> 01:38:04.301
Then I went to C2 . . . I went to basic . . . I went to A.I.T. training.

1418
01:38:04.301 --> 01:38:06.407
I forgot what number that was.

1419
01:38:06.407 --> 01:38:14.483
Then I went to drill sergeant school, then I trained troops in the same company, I mean same unit, until I got out.

1420
01:38:14.483 --> 01:38:19.048
But I was there with the body counts was coming in from Vietnam.

1421
01:38:19.048 --> 01:38:21.857
You could see them on television every day.

1422
01:38:21.857 --> 01:38:24.666
The different body counts that was coming in.

1423
01:38:24.666 --> 01:38:33.093
I got angry and I went told the company commander I wanted to go to Vietnam because of what had happened with his stripe,

1424
01:38:33.093 --> 01:38:40.817
and he told me to get out of his office because if he wanted me in Vietnam I'd have been in Vietnam.

1425
01:38:40.817 --> 01:38:45.031
So I just went on and start . . . I kept doing my duty,

1426
01:38:45.031 --> 01:38:48.191
but I didn’t . . . My heart wasn't in it anymore.

1427
01:38:48.191 --> 01:38:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about basic training?

1428
01:38:51.000 --> 01:38:56.686
<v DELMA BENNETT>Basic training was kind of scary a little because when

1429
01:38:56.686 --> 01:39:02.941
<c.p>we went in, Fort Ord was closed down for a while.

1430
01:39:02.941 --> 01:39:09.765
It was closed down because of that disease that was going on.

1431
01:39:09.765 --> 01:39:12.039
What is it called?

1432
01:39:12.039 --> 01:39:14.882
When your neck draw back?

1433
01:39:14.882 --> 01:39:16.588
<v ARSENEAULT>I don't know.

1434
01:39:16.588 --> 01:39:18.294
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah, it's meningitis.

1435
01:39:18.294 --> 01:39:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

1436
01:39:20.000 --> 01:39:27.639
<v DELMA BENNETT>They had a bad meningitis outbreak at Fort Ord and they had closed Fort Ord down for a while.

1437
01:39:27.639 --> 01:39:31.258
I don't know if it was from the breathing.

1438
01:39:31.258 --> 01:39:35.680
I don't know what it was, but we couldn't have heaters.

1439
01:39:35.680 --> 01:39:38.495
We couldn't have heaters in the barracks.

1440
01:39:38.495 --> 01:39:40.505
All the windows stayed open.

1441
01:39:40.505 --> 01:39:47.340
If you needed to get warm you had to get extra blankets and stuff to keep warm.

1442
01:39:47.340 --> 01:39:50.557
We were right on the peninsula of Monterey . . .

1443
01:39:50.557 --> 01:39:51.361
<v ARSENEAULT>Monterey Bay.

1444
01:39:51.361 --> 01:39:51.763
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah.

1445
01:39:51.763 --> 01:39:54.979
So that's the only thing about basic that . . .

1446
01:39:54.979 --> 01:39:59.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh it happened while you were in basic training?

1447
01:39:59.000 --> 01:40:00.503
<v DELMA BENNETT>Not the meningitis outbreak.

1448
01:40:00.503 --> 01:40:03.886
The meningitis outbreak took place before I got there.

1449
01:40:03.886 --> 01:40:04.262
<v ARSENEAULT>Before.

1450
01:40:04.262 --> 01:40:06.893
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah, but . . . And the base was closed.

1451
01:40:06.893 --> 01:40:07.268
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1452
01:40:07.268 --> 01:40:09.899
<v DELMA BENNETT>So they reopened the base in '65.

1453
01:40:09.899 --> 01:40:17.040
When we got there they reopened the base for training, and we went in right after they reopened it.

1454
01:40:17.040 --> 01:40:20.423
And then there weren't any more outbreaks after that.

1455
01:40:20.423 --> 01:40:28.315
I don't know if it was because of the procedures they went through because the barracks weren’t heated up or anything.

1456
01:40:28.315 --> 01:40:33.953
They kept the barracks cold because it get cold sometimes at night on that peninsula.

1457
01:40:33.953 --> 01:40:38.087
I did two years there and it was a beautiful tour.

1458
01:40:38.087 --> 01:40:39.215
It was beautiful.

1459
01:40:39.215 --> 01:40:41.846
I mean I don't have no complaints.

1460
01:40:41.846 --> 01:40:48.987
After I got a little rank and stuff I lived off base in Seaside with me and my friend.

1461
01:40:48.987 --> 01:40:50.866
It was a good experience.

1462
01:40:50.866 --> 01:40:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did they teach you in basic?

1463
01:40:55.000 --> 01:40:56.724
<v DELMA BENNETT>They teach us drilling.

1464
01:40:56.724 --> 01:40:59.740
First of all they teach us drilling.

1465
01:40:59.740 --> 01:41:01.894
The proper way of aligning.

1466
01:41:01.894 --> 01:41:07.496
That’s falling in formation and knowing your different moves and what have you.

1467
01:41:07.496 --> 01:41:11.374
Then after drilling then they taught us our weapons.

1468
01:41:11.374 --> 01:41:16.545
We was using M-14s at the time before the M-16 came in.

1469
01:41:16.545 --> 01:41:20.423
They taught us how to break the weapons down.

1470
01:41:20.423 --> 01:41:25.593
They . . . the 45s, we had to learn how to use the 45s.

1471
01:41:25.593 --> 01:41:28.610
Then we had to go to training.

1472
01:41:28.610 --> 01:41:35.073
We had to go to different trainings and we had to run to these trainings.

1473
01:41:35.073 --> 01:41:42.398
They would have different areas where they would teach you about your M.O.S. . . . whatever your M.O.S. was,

1474
01:41:42.398 --> 01:41:45.415
they would train you on how you

1475
01:41:45.415 --> 01:41:46.276
<c.p>have to . . .

1476
01:41:46.276 --> 01:41:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was yours?

1477
01:41:48.000 --> 01:41:49.091
<v DELMA BENNETT>I was 11C-Charlie.

1478
01:41:49.091 --> 01:41:50.909
That was a Mortar Man.

1479
01:41:50.909 --> 01:41:56.364
That's where you drop this big shell into a tube and it [explosion sound effect].

1480
01:41:56.364 --> 01:41:58.182
I wasn’t on the frontline.

1481
01:41:58.182 --> 01:42:01.818
I don't know if it was my education or what . . .

1482
01:42:01.818 --> 01:42:04.364
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that what you learned in A.I.T.?

1483
01:42:04.364 --> 01:42:04.727
<v DELMA BENNETT>Huh?

1484
01:42:04.727 --> 01:42:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that what you learned at your A.I.T.?

1485
01:42:08.000 --> 01:42:10.422
<v DELMA BENNETT>That's what we learned in A.I.T., yeah.

1486
01:42:10.422 --> 01:42:14.227
That's when we got the A.I.T. but we did basics training.

1487
01:42:14.227 --> 01:42:15.611
We did hand-to-hand combat.

1488
01:42:15.611 --> 01:42:18.379
We did . . . In basic that's what we did.

1489
01:42:18.379 --> 01:42:19.071
Hand-to-hand combat.

1490
01:42:19.071 --> 01:42:20.455
How to fire weapons.

1491
01:42:20.455 --> 01:42:25.299
We had to go to the rifle range and all that kind of stuff.

1492
01:42:25.299 --> 01:42:31.526
We had to qualify to find out how good we were with the rifle and what have you.

1493
01:42:31.526 --> 01:42:32.910
I weren't never expert.

1494
01:42:32.910 --> 01:42:36.370
I was always a marksman, and that's right under expert.

1495
01:42:36.370 --> 01:42:40.521
I mean I wasn't expert because I never used a gun before.

1496
01:42:40.521 --> 01:42:49.171
I had never had no experience with guns, and guns never excited me so it didn't really make me any difference about using the gun.

1497
01:42:49.171 --> 01:42:51.592
They taught us to be a unit.

1498
01:42:51.592 --> 01:43:02.664
To be able to love one another regardless of who we were because we never would have known . . . We never knew if we would ever go to Vietnam or what have you.

1499
01:43:02.664 --> 01:43:05.777
<c.p>So they had to teach us the buddy system.

1500
01:43:05.777 --> 01:43:14.081
To watch out and to care for one another because we don't know if who would have helped us in any situation and stuff.

1501
01:43:14.081 --> 01:43:16.156
Army was a very good experience.

1502
01:43:16.156 --> 01:43:17.540
I loved it man.

1503
01:43:17.540 --> 01:43:20.308
You have three square meals a day. [laughs]

1504
01:43:20.308 --> 01:43:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Chow.

1505
01:43:21.000 --> 01:43:21.356
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yeah.

1506
01:43:21.356 --> 01:43:25.271
Three square meals a day and you eat all you want.

1507
01:43:25.271 --> 01:43:26.339
It was nice.

1508
01:43:26.339 --> 01:43:27.763
It was really nice.

1509
01:43:27.763 --> 01:43:30.610
We had competitions with the barracks and stuff.

1510
01:43:30.610 --> 01:43:39.508
Who would have the best barracks with the . . . When you leave out in the mornings and stuff and how you had all your stuff displayed.

1511
01:43:39.508 --> 01:43:42.000
Everything was displayed, oh it was nice.

1512
01:43:42.000 --> 01:43:44.492
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have to shine your boots?

1513
01:43:44.492 --> 01:43:45.203
<v DELMA BENNETT>Oh yeah.

1514
01:43:45.203 --> 01:43:48.407
I was a shoe shine man from the beginning.

1515
01:43:48.407 --> 01:43:53.034
That was my first entrepreneur job shining shoes right here in Lake Charles.

1516
01:43:53.034 --> 01:43:55.525
<v ARSENEAULT>So you had one up on everybody?

1517
01:43:55.525 --> 01:43:56.949
<v DELMA BENNETT>Oh, I had that.

1518
01:43:56.949 --> 01:44:02.644
I definitely had that because I was . . . I had me a business at seven years old.

1519
01:44:02.644 --> 01:44:10.475
I would walk from my house all the way to Highway 14 shining shoes at all the different businesses on Broad Street.

1520
01:44:10.475 --> 01:44:16.169
At the end of the day I had me seven or eight dollars in my pocket.

1521
01:44:16.169 --> 01:44:17.593
Fifteen cents a shine.

1522
01:44:17.593 --> 01:44:21.153
So you know I must have been doing something good.

1523
01:44:21.153 --> 01:44:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What would you do with the money?

1524
01:44:24.000 --> 01:44:25.828
<v DELMA BENNETT>I give it to my grandmother.

1525
01:44:25.828 --> 01:44:27.656
I give it to my grandmother.

1526
01:44:27.656 --> 01:44:29.483
<v ARSENEAULT>You didn't buy candy with it?

1527
01:44:29.483 --> 01:44:32.225
<v DELMA BENNETT>I’d buy me a little snack when I'm working.

1528
01:44:32.225 --> 01:44:39.841
I might stop over and buy a little something, but at the end of the day I’d just give her what I earned and stuff.

1529
01:44:39.841 --> 01:44:42.887
Until one day I asked her . . . I wasn't working then.

1530
01:44:42.887 --> 01:44:47.762
I asked her to give me fifty cents to go to the movie and she said,

1531
01:44:47.762 --> 01:44:51.417
"I can't give you no fifty cents to go to no movie."

1532
01:44:51.417 --> 01:44:59.642
She said, "If I give you fifty cents to go to the movie I got to give all my grandkids fifty cents to go to the movie."

1533
01:44:59.642 --> 01:45:02.384
She didn't get no more money from me then.

1534
01:45:02.384 --> 01:45:04.821
That was the last time getting some money.

1535
01:45:04.821 --> 01:45:05.126
Yep.

1536
01:45:05.126 --> 01:45:08.477
<v ARSENEAULT>So besides Drill Sergeant Gentry, do you remember any other ones?

1537
01:45:08.477 --> 01:45:08.781
<v DELMA BENNETT>Sergeant . . .

1538
01:45:08.781 --> 01:45:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Any memorable ones?

1539
01:45:10.000 --> 01:45:10.773
<v DELMA BENNETT>Yes sergeant . . .

1540
01:45:10.773 --> 01:45:11.933
<v ARSENEAULT>Any mean ones?

1541
01:45:11.933 --> 01:45:14.253
<v DELMA BENNETT>I didn't have no mean ones.

1542
01:45:14.253 --> 01:45:18.507
I mean I was a . . . I always had humor in me.

1543
01:45:18.507 --> 01:45:24.307
Even when something would upset me I would use my humor to calm me down.

1544
01:45:24.307 --> 01:45:26.240
They had [Riddley?], Sergeant Riddley.

1545
01:45:26.240 --> 01:45:30.880
He was . . . He wound up being part of the family, Sergeant Riddley.

1546
01:45:30.880 --> 01:45:33.587
Yeah, he married one of my cousins.

1547
01:45:33.587 --> 01:45:39.773
Then Sergeant Williams, this was another guy, his name was . . . He wasn't no drill sergeant though.

1548
01:45:39.773 --> 01:45:43.640
But he wore the brown hat and what have you.

1549
01:45:43.640 --> 01:45:51.373
Just . . . You had to go to school to become a drill sergeant and he didn't go to drill sergeant school,

1550
01:45:51.373 --> 01:45:55.240
but he still was in charge of troops and stuff.

1551
01:45:55.240 --> 01:45:59.493
That's my main . . . That's the main three that I dealt with.

1552
01:45:59.493 --> 01:46:03.747
Gentry, and Williams and I, we call kind of ran together.

1553
01:46:03.747 --> 01:46:13.800
But Sergeant Gentry, he was there way before I even got into any rank, but I didn't see him no more after I got into rank.

1554
01:46:13.800 --> 01:46:16.120
Then I met other sergeants, too.

1555
01:46:16.120 --> 01:46:19.213
I got pictures of all of them somewhere.

1556
01:46:19.213 --> 01:46:21.920
I was taking pictures at that time.

1557
01:46:21.920 --> 01:46:30.040
I would take pictures of our training and stuff and I would go to the dark room and have them printed.

1558
01:46:30.040 --> 01:46:34.293
I would sell them and stuff to the troops and stuff.

1559
01:46:34.293 --> 01:46:35.067
I did . . .

1560
01:46:35.067 --> 01:46:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You were a hustler.

1561
01:46:37.000 --> 01:46:40.913
<v DELMA BENNETT>I've always been a . . . I have a lawn service now.

1562
01:46:40.913 --> 01:46:47.565
I just didn't do it today because you guys was coming, but I've always been an entrepreneur.

1563
01:46:47.565 --> 01:46:52.261
Because I . . . It's just hard for me to depend on other people.

1564
01:46:52.261 --> 01:46:53.435
I've had jobs.

1565
01:46:53.435 --> 01:46:56.174
I've had . . . I worked at the plants.

1566
01:46:56.174 --> 01:47:02.043
And when I got to the plant . . . It's so strange about working at these refineries.

1567
01:47:02.043 --> 01:47:07.130
You actually feel, because you work at those refineries, that you have arrived.

1568
01:47:07.130 --> 01:47:08.304
Isn't that strange?

1569
01:47:08.304 --> 01:47:13.783
Because these are the best paying jobs in this area; working at the plants.

1570
01:47:13.783 --> 01:47:18.087
My . . . They never told me that, but they told my brother-in-law.

1571
01:47:18.087 --> 01:47:24.348
He said, "Look you can come to this plant, and you can work at this plant."

1572
01:47:24.348 --> 01:47:30.609
And he said, "If you work at this plant and handle your money right by the

1573
01:47:30.609 --> 01:47:34.130
<c.p>time that you retire you can become a millionaire."

1574
01:47:34.130 --> 01:47:36.870
A lot of them have done this.

1575
01:47:36.870 --> 01:47:40.000
The ones that use their money wisely.

1576
01:47:40.000 --> 01:47:46.593
He said, "But I can promise you this, that will take at least ten years off your life."

1577
01:47:46.593 --> 01:47:50.622
What a person that work at a plant would tell you,

1578
01:47:50.622 --> 01:47:56.483
"I'd rather die with money in my pocket than to die without money in my pocket.”

1579
01:47:56.483 --> 01:48:04.907
So I can endure what I'm going through at the plant knowing that I can do almost anything that I want to do.

1580
01:48:04.907 --> 01:48:10.401
I got vacation every year, I can buy me a new car every three years,

1581
01:48:10.401 --> 01:48:15.163
I can buy me a block-long house before I get out of there.

1582
01:48:15.163 --> 01:48:20.291
And they don't have statistics showing exactly what the people from the plants died.

1583
01:48:20.291 --> 01:48:26.884
But normally if a person work at the plant all their life, at least thirty to forty years,

1584
01:48:26.884 --> 01:48:32.012
when they leave out of that plant they will not last over seven years.

1585
01:48:32.012 --> 01:48:35.308
They would die within a period of seven years.

1586
01:48:35.308 --> 01:48:40.436
Some longer now, but the majority would die within a period of seven years.

1587
01:48:40.436 --> 01:48:43.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What happens to their retirement money?

1588
01:48:43.000 --> 01:48:47.364
<v DELMA BENNETT>Well I mean it . . . I don't know if it stop or not,

1589
01:48:47.364 --> 01:48:55.727
but I guess that it might be set up for their . . . Oh yeah you can set up for your wife to get it.

1590
01:48:55.727 --> 01:49:04.818
You see, just like when I retired from Firestone I don't get but fifty-eight dollars a month from Firestone because I only worked ten years.

1591
01:49:04.818 --> 01:49:13.182
I would have gotten a hundred and sixty-five dollars if I would have waited until I was sixty-five, but I left at fifty-five.

1592
01:49:13.182 --> 01:49:15.364
So I only got fifty-eight dollars.

1593
01:49:15.364 --> 01:49:25.182
And I would have got sixty-some dollars if I would have set that I wanted my wife to get the fifty-eight dollars if something happen to me.

1594
01:49:25.182 --> 01:49:32.091
So they got it set up so if something happen to them, then their wife would get their retirement.

1595
01:49:32.091 --> 01:49:35.727
That's what I've learned from my experience at the plant.

1596
01:49:35.727 --> 01:49:40.818
But I really thought that I had arrived when I got to the plant.

1597
01:49:40.818 --> 01:49:43.364
I mean I was able to do

1598
01:49:43.364 --> 01:49:45.182
<c.p>what I wanted to do.

1599
01:49:45.182 --> 01:49:51.727
I was taking my kids to Disney World and all . . . Disney Land and all that kind of stuff.

1600
01:49:51.727 --> 01:49:56.455
I would buy me a car every three years and stuff like that.

1601
01:49:56.455 --> 01:49:58.636
But I only worked ten years.

1602
01:49:58.636 --> 01:50:01.909
But I didn't do like the rest of them.

1603
01:50:01.909 --> 01:50:08.091
When I got there I knew the biggest purchase a man had to make was a home.

1604
01:50:08.091 --> 01:50:16.091
So I devoted my time in making enough money where I could buy my home and wouldn't have a mortgage on it.

1605
01:50:16.091 --> 01:50:19.000
So I just paid cash for my home.

1606
01:50:19.000 --> 01:50:20.455
Just like this home.

1607
01:50:20.455 --> 01:50:22.273
I just can't stand note.

1608
01:50:22.273 --> 01:50:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Debt?

1609
01:50:23.000 --> 01:50:23.316
<v DELMA BENNETT>Ma'am?

1610
01:50:23.316 --> 01:50:23.949
<v ARSENEAULT>The debt?

1611
01:50:23.949 --> 01:50:26.165
<v DELMA BENNETT>I just can't stand a note period.

1612
01:50:26.165 --> 01:50:28.380
I hate paying utility bills every month.

1613
01:50:28.380 --> 01:50:34.392
But I got to pay that because I can't just pay one lump sum and be done with it.

1614
01:50:34.392 --> 01:50:40.089
Man, to know I'm tied down to a note where I got to pay mortgage and all that.

1615
01:50:40.089 --> 01:50:43.253
I hate paying the insurance on my cars and stuff.

1616
01:50:43.253 --> 01:50:44.835
Especially life insurance and stuff.

1617
01:50:44.835 --> 01:50:54.013
So I got a life insurance where I just pay it one time and that took care of what I had to do if anything happened to my wife.

1618
01:50:54.013 --> 01:51:00.342
I ain't got nothing on me right now, but I put enough money on the side just to bury me.

1619
01:51:00.342 --> 01:51:05.089
If we ever sell Mossville I'm going to just let my wife have all that.

1620
01:51:05.089 --> 01:51:06.671
Whatever we get from Mossville.

1621
01:51:06.671 --> 01:51:08.570
I don't need none of that.

1622
01:51:08.570 --> 01:51:10.785
<v ARSENEAULT>What was your first impression of Mossville?

1623
01:51:10.785 --> 01:51:13.000
When did you first go there?

1624
01:51:13.000 --> 01:51:15.492
<v DELMA BENNETT>My first impression was my wife.

1625
01:51:15.492 --> 01:51:17.569
<v ARSENEAULT>That's all you saw, huh?

1626
01:51:17.569 --> 01:51:20.476
<v DELMA BENNETT>I didn't care nothing about no Mossville.

1627
01:51:20.476 --> 01:51:22.968
See I played ball against Mossville.

1628
01:51:22.968 --> 01:51:26.706
I used to go to football games and stuff.

1629
01:51:26.706 --> 01:51:33.351
Mossville always had a good athletic department because they had some great athletes that came through.

1630
01:51:33.351 --> 01:51:39.581
Some of them was professional football players and basketball players that came out of Mossville.

1631
01:51:39.581 --> 01:51:42.488
Guy by the name of Johnny Comeaux.

1632
01:51:42.488 --> 01:51:45.395
He played for the New Orleans Jazz.

1633
01:51:45.395 --> 01:51:47.056
They had Richard Lee.

1634
01:51:47.056 --> 01:51:49.133
Not Richard Lee, his brother.

1635
01:51:49.133 --> 01:51:50.794
He played professional football.

1636
01:51:50.794 --> 01:51:58.685
He had went to Grambling State and got a scholarship in professional football and all that kind of stuff.

1637
01:51:58.685 --> 01:52:02.839
So they had a few other ones that went through.

1638
01:52:02.839 --> 01:52:05.331
The Kelly boys was real good.

1639
01:52:05.331 --> 01:52:12.806
And bunch of them was . . . The Payne boys was good in basketball and they had an outstanding team.

1640
01:52:12.806 --> 01:52:14.468
Often they had that.

1641
01:52:14.468 --> 01:52:16.544
That was good about Mossville.

1642
01:52:16.544 --> 01:52:24.020
But just Mossville itself, the only reason why I wound up in Mossville was because of my wife.

1643
01:52:24.020 --> 01:52:33.988
Because I'm from the Boulevard area of Lake Charles, and that was a prominent black area that I lived around here in Lake Charles.

1644
01:52:33.988 --> 01:52:40.633
So the only thing that really hooked me to Mossville, like I said was my wife.

1645
01:52:40.633 --> 01:52:48.524
When I bought my home I bought it in Mossville so I just stayed there raising my children there.

1646
01:52:48.524 --> 01:52:53.508
See I have four kids and they were raised in Mossville, so.

1647
01:52:53.508 --> 01:52:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all have a garden?

1648
01:52:56.000 --> 01:52:57.545
<v DELMA BENNETT>I'm from the city.

1649
01:52:57.545 --> 01:53:00.634
What I'm going to do with a garden?

1650
01:53:00.634 --> 01:53:02.178
I'm from San Francisco.

1651
01:53:02.178 --> 01:53:04.495
<v ARSENEAULT>You can garden in San Francisco.

1652
01:53:04.495 --> 01:53:06.426
<v DELMA BENNETT>No there ain't . . . A garden?

1653
01:53:06.426 --> 01:53:08.743
What I want with a garden?

1654
01:53:08.743 --> 01:53:09.901
No, I didn’t.

1655
01:53:09.901 --> 01:53:14.149
I tried that one time, but that don't work for me.

1656
01:53:14.149 --> 01:53:16.465
<v ARSENEAULT>It didn't work out for you?

1657
01:53:16.465 --> 01:53:17.624
<v DELMA BENNETT>Not for me.

1658
01:53:17.624 --> 01:53:19.168
I'm from the city.

1659
01:53:19.168 --> 01:53:21.485
I ain't looking for no garden.

1660
01:53:21.485 --> 01:53:26.119
I tried to do a horse one time and couldn’t do it.

1661
01:53:26.119 --> 01:53:31.911
I did a poor goat, man and . . . I ain't going to talk about my goat.

1662
01:53:31.911 --> 01:53:33.842
<v ARSENEAULT>What happened to your goat?

1663
01:53:33.842 --> 01:53:36.158
<v DELMA BENNETT>My goat fell in a hole.

1664
01:53:36.158 --> 01:53:39.634
Both of his front paws fell in a hole.

1665
01:53:39.634 --> 01:53:41.950
I'm looking everywhere for my goat.

1666
01:53:41.950 --> 01:53:45.426
My goat in a hole freezing to death man.

1667
01:53:45.426 --> 01:53:48.129
That hurt me to my heart man.

1668
01:53:48.129 --> 01:53:50.059
Then I had a chicken.

1669
01:53:50.059 --> 01:54:02.030
I had a chicken on time and a . . . Mean old rooster and he had spurs and I caught myself going to cut his spurs and made that chicken bleed to death.

1670
01:54:02.030 --> 01:54:03.188
Man, no farmer.

1671
01:54:03.188 --> 01:54:06.663
I mean I didn't to do it on purpose.

1672
01:54:06.663 --> 01:54:07.822
Don't be laughing.

1673
01:54:07.822 --> 01:54:10.139
I didn't do it on purpose.

1674
01:54:10.139 --> 01:54:10.911
Messing around . . .

1675
01:54:10.911 --> 01:54:12.069
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>Y'all still there?

1676
01:54:12.069 --> 01:54:14.000
<v DELMA BENNETT>Talking about a garden.

1677
